 official store phones 2026"], "request_id": "d3ef9383-63e5-4e46-8aea-1eaf22aebc3a", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_model_queries": {"queries": ["Xiaomi phones US compatibility T-Mobile 2026 Xiaomi 15 Ultra bands", "Xiaomi US official store phones 2026"], "type": "search_model_queries"}, "thinking_effort": "standard", "tool_icons": ["globe"], "turn_exchange_id": "86f0db74-6bbc-428c-98f5-642994b2c0e7", "working_turn_id": "86f0db74-6bbc-428c-98f5-642994b2c0e7"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": null, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {"real_author": "tool:web"}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Yep — if by “MiMo phone” you mean a **Xiaomi phone**, there are some genuinely interesting options. The important catch is that Xiaomi still doesn’t sell its main phone lineup through the normal U.S. carrier retail channel, so we’d be buying an **unlocked global/international model** and validating it against T-Mobile before committing. Xiaomi’s U.S. site exists, but the global phone catalog is where the actual handset lineup lives. citeturn608596search1turn608596search2\n\nFor your use case, I’d look at these three:\n\nproducts{\"selections\":[[\"turn608596product2\",\"Xiaomi 15 5G Global / International\"],[\"turn608596product1\",\"Xiaomi 15T Pro Global\"],[\"turn608596product0\",\"Xiaomi 15 Ultra Global\"]]}\n\n**Xiaomi 15** is the one I’d investigate first. It’s a true flagship with Snapdragon 8 Elite, 12 GB RAM, Leica cameras, and it’s relatively compact. I’m seeing a current unlocked international listing around **$700**, which is a hell of a lot more rational than spending $1,200+ just to experiment with Xiaomi. citeturn608596product2\n\nThe **15T Pro** is the bigger/value-oriented option: 12 GB RAM, 512 GB storage, large 144 Hz AMOLED, and Leica camera hardware. Current global-version listings are roughly **$740–$900** depending on seller. citeturn608596product1turn608596product4\n\nThe **15 Ultra** is the lunatic-camera-phone option. Snapdragon 8 Elite, 16/512 GB, serious Leica camera system, but current global versions are around **$1,200+**. That feels excessive for what we’re trying to accomplish unless you specifically want the camera monster. citeturn608596product0\n\n### The T-Mobile question is the important part\n\nThe **global Xiaomi 15 Ultra** is actually encouraging: Xiaomi lists LTE bands **2/4/5/12/41/66/71** and 5G including **n25/n41/n66/n71**, among many others. Those are highly relevant T-Mobile bands. citeturn608596search0 Third-party compatibility data similarly shows the global 15 Ultra covering all seven of the LTE bands it tracks for T-Mobile and six of nine tracked 5G bands. citeturn608596search3\n\nBut band support ≠ carrier certification. **Before buying an imported Xiaomi, I would verify the exact IMEI with T-Mobile's BYOD checker**, because T-Mobile itself uses the IMEI/EID to determine whether a device can actually activate. citeturn608596search5\n\nAnd crucially: **buy GLOBAL, not Chinese-market firmware.** The global variants generally have materially better U.S. band coverage. For example, the global Xiaomi 15 supports more of T-Mobile's tracked 5G bands than the China model. citeturn608596search8\n\nMy target for us would therefore be:\n\n**Xiaomi 15 Global, 12 GB / 512 GB, unlocked — around $600–$700.**\n\nThat gives you the actual Xiaomi/HyperOS ecosystem, flagship hardware, and a fun connection to the same company whose **MiMo models we're using in GCE**, without spending Ultra money.\n\nAnd yes, there is something wonderfully GCE about **running MiMo on a Xiaomi while using the phone as the mobile front end to DevPad.** 😄"]}, "create_time": 1790217985.381345, "end_turn": true, "id": "2dba6174-8a25-482b-842e-878e47682a22", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [{"alt": "([Mi Poland](https://www.mi.com/us?ut