ivacy display, priced similarly to Apple's flagship. \n\nFor budget users, the $499 Google Pixel 10a offers excellent value with solid camera performance, durable design, and long battery life. Gamers and power users seeking top battery longevity should consider the Asus ROG Phone 9 Pro, which lasts over 20 hours but comes at a hefty $1,100. The Google Pixel 10 and iPhone 17 provide strong flagship performance at a more accessible $799, with the former excelling in AI tools and camera upgrades and the latter narrowing the gap with Apple’s Pro models. Samsung's Galaxy Z Fold 8 Ultra leads the foldable category with improved design and internals, despite its steep $2,099 starting price.", "thumbnail_url": "https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-1/lmJ5NVXkMtmFGlaThWIM7W4l7vWDkXOXIddWWc8BRQDkZB41b7RjKm3Nkf3-3bokrVp0hugprynHqgNPlraPBMhYYdXp2LDQ5w2BdzWt6DUww3KltIOBzLnJXvYJ7kb-", "title": "Best phones 2026 tested - our top picks", "type": "search_result", "url": "https://www.tomsguide.com/best-picks/best-phones"}, {"attribution": "www.tomsguide.com", "pub_date": 1790007623, "ref_id": {"ref_index": 13, "ref_type": "news", "turn_index": 743415}, "snippet": "As of 2026, gaming phones offer powerful alternatives to consoles and PCs, providing robust performance, long battery life, and high-refresh-rate displays for immersive gameplay. The Asus ROG Phone 9 Pro tops the list as the best overall gaming phone, with a 185Hz AMOLED display, Snapdragon 8 Elite chipset, exceptional 20:34 battery life, and gaming-specific features like AirTrigger controls and AniMe lights. For Android users wanting a flagship device with strong gaming credentials, the Samsung Galaxy S26 Ultra delivers with excellent GPU performance, efficient cooling, and powerful cameras. Meanwhile, the iPhone 18 Pro Max is the best gaming iPhone, offering the A20 Pro chip and impressive battery life, though with a heftier price tag. For value-conscious gamers, the RedMagic 11 Pro brings liquid and fan cooling and top-tier specs at a lower cost, albeit with weaker camera and dust resistance. Finally, the Galaxy Z Fold 7 offers a unique foldable experience with a large display and high-end internals, though at $2,000 it overdelivers for pure gaming needs. Overall, the best choice depends on user priorities such as OS, performance, budget, and additional features like photography or portability.", "thumbnail_url": "https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-1/3YBCBMfoBpyNpGm5vWKT_02-GdnjWkB5ZHVmQOv4it31Yb8XZCCr0eThXZatldRUs_gg1eAzWIh1GqA_WeZmGHZnumQQDJgwHnX4lcNj2mIRuIJt7rsCXVX9lAcQ9h1Z", "title": "The best gaming phone 2026 - I tested them all to crown a winner", "type": "search_result", "url": "https://www.tomsguide.com/best-picks/best-gaming-phones"}, {"attribution": "www.tomsguide.com", "pub_date": 1790003659, "ref_id": {"ref_index": 15, "ref_type": "news", "turn_index": 743415}, "snippet": "If you're in the market for a large smartphone, this guide highlights the best big phones of 2026, ideal for users who prioritize spacious displays, extended battery life, and top-tier performance. The best overall is the Samsung Galaxy S26 Ultra, boasting a 6.9-inch AMOLED screen, Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 chip, and robust features like customizable privacy and AI tools. It stands out for exceptional performance and camera quality, despite minor drawbacks like its large camera bump.\n\nFor Apple fans, the iPhone 18 Pro Max also features a 6.9-inch display, A20 Pro chip, and superior battery life (18:27 hours), making it the top choice among big iPhones. It shines with strong performance, advanced camera capabilities, and fast charging, albeit with a higher price and heftier build.\n\nIf you're seeking flexibility, the foldable Motorola Razr Fold offers an expansive 8.1-inch inner screen and strong multitasking abilities. It supports up to four simultaneous apps and delivers solid battery life despite a few software hiccups.\n\nUltimately, Android offers more diversity in design and features, while 