ing_effort": "standard", "tool_icons": ["https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=help.openai.com&sz=128"], "turn_exchange_id": "ad46b190-46f1-4174-b394-77a2fc96aaed", "working_turn_id": "ad46b190-46f1-4174-b394-77a2fc96aaed"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": null, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {"real_author": "tool:web.run"}, "name": "web.run", "role": "tool"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": [""]}, "create_time": 1790228794.5048983, "end_turn": null, "id": "94a89066-b46b-4a14-bea1-33bd51217f59", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "chime_version": 1, "classifier_response": "default", "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "gizmo_id": "g-p-6ab41b7c2620819187c626358eeb8a48", "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "parent_id": "4fe5cbc4-31f3-4ede-addc-0e3bf7e89640", "reasoning_start_time": 1790228789.9636593, "reasoning_status": "is_reasoning", "reasoning_title": "Searching the web", "reasoning_titles": ["Searching the web"], "request_id": "8ad38e31-0ca8-49cb-b133-76727e48581a", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_model_queries": {"queries": ["site:openai.com chatgpt content-security-policy frame-ancestors iframe", "site:help.openai.com ChatGPT browser login cookies session browser"], "type": "search_model_queries"}, "thinking_effort": "standard", "tool_icons": ["globe"], "turn_exchange_id": "ad46b190-46f1-4174-b394-77a2fc96aaed", "working_turn_id": "ad46b190-46f1-4174-b394-77a2fc96aaed"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": null, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {"real_author": "tool:web"}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Yep — this is almost certainly **not a ChatGPT login problem anymore**. It’s an **embedding-model problem**.\n\nYou can see the distinction in your screenshot:\n\n- Coda has the correct seat tile.\n- The URL is correct: `https://chatgpt.com/`.\n- The tile says `seat: stopped`.\n- There is no embedded browser surface inside the pane.\n\nThat usually means Nico is trying to treat a real desktop browser like a terminal/service process, when the DevPad itself is just a browser app. A Chrome or Brave window does **not** naturally render *inside another browser page*.\n\n## Why this is harder than the terminals\n\nThe terminal panes are easy because DevPad owns them:\n\n**DevPad → node-pty → process → xterm.js**\n\nThat gives DevPad a stream it can render directly.\n\nA real browser is different:\n\n**DevPad browser page → Chrome/Brave desktop process**\n\nLaunching Chrome gives you a **separate native window**. It does not hand DevPad an embeddable DOM/surface.\n\nThere are basically three technical approaches:\n\n1. **iframe ChatGPT into the DevPad pane**  \n   Easiest conceptually, but many authenticated/security-sensitive sites prevent being framed through browser security policy. I would not architect around this.\n\n2. **Launch Brave/Chrome externally and make the tile a launcher/focus controller**  \n   This is the easiest and most reliable near-term solution. The Coda tile says `RUNNING`, shows the browser PID/window handle, and **Launch / Focus** brings your already-authenticated Brave profile forward. Your login remains in the persistent browser profile. OpenAI itself distinguishes between a browser with its own session state versus using an existing Chrome profile precisely because the profile carries cookies and signed-in sessions. citeturn248178search1turn248178search3\n\n3. **Build an actual embedded browser host**  \n   If you truly want ChatGPT rendered *inside* that rectangle, DevPad needs something like an **Electron/WebView2/native host architecture**, not just ordinary HTML. That is a bigger architectural move.\n\n### I think we were solving the wrong problem\n\nFor **Coda v1**, I would **not embed ChatGPT**.\n\nMake Coda a **managed external browser seat**:\n\n```text\nCoda tile\n  ↓\npersistent Brave profile\n  ↓\nchatgpt.com\n  ↓\nDon logged in normally\n  ↓\nDevPad tracks PID/HWND\n  ↓\nLaunch / Focus\n```\n\nThen the tile can look like:\n\n```text\n[1] Coda — ChatGPT\nBrowser: Brave\nStatus: RUNNING\nAccount session: persistent\nPID: 21636\nHWND: 1188472\n\n[ Focus Coda ]\n```\n\nThat gets you almost everything you actually need with **very little engineering risk**.\n\nAnd the login part you already did is correct: use a persistent normal browser profile. OpenAI's current browser guidance also treats signed-in browser state as distinct and persistent, and recommends entering credentials in the browser rather than handing them through another application. citeturn248178search1\n\n### Why it currently says `seat: stopped`\n\nMy bet is one of these:\n\n- Nico's browser adapter starts Chrome/Brave and immediately loses process ownership because the browser hands off to an already-running browser process.\n- The adapter expects the process it spawned to remain alive, but Chrome/Brave reuse an existing browser process and the launcher exits.\n- HWND discovery isn't finding the existing browser window/profile.\n- The configured executable/profile isn't the same Brave instance you manually logged into.\n- The seat status logic only understands **spawned child PID = running**, which is fine for OpenCode but wrong for browsers.\n\nThat last one is especially likely.\n\nSo I would tell Nico:\n\n**Stop trying to make Coda behave like an OpenCode pane. Browser seats need different lifecycle semantics.**\n\nFor browsers:\n\n**RUNNING = matching authenticated-profile browser/window exists**,  \nnot **the original spawned process is still my child**.\n\nIf you want, I can give you a tight **Nico browser-seat corrective prompt** that specifically tells him to abandon iframe embedding for now and make Brave a persistent managed external seat with PID/HWND focus."]}, "create_time": 1790228795.073386, "end_turn": true, "id": "