"Yep. Here’s the Luce recon prompt in one clean copy box.\n\n```text\nGCE myDon CONTROL — PHASE 0 INBOX / MCP RECON\n\nAuthority: Don\nSeat: Luce\nMode: READ-ONLY RECON / GROUND TRUTH\nPrimary Domain: GCE Stack\nTarget Workstream: myDon Control / DevPad Command Center\nBuild Status: BUILD HOLD — RECON ONLY\nPurpose: Determine exactly what the existing inbox / ai-inbox / inbox MCP can already do so myDon does not create a redundant queue or messaging system.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDo not build myDon.\nDo not modify DevPad.\nDo not change runtime bindings.\nDo not edit inbox files unless explicitly authorized later.\nDo not create new storage.\nDo not redesign the inbox.\nDo not normalize schemas.\nDo not infer capabilities from filenames or comments.\n\nYour job is to inspect the current implementation and establish what the existing inbox system ACTUALLY supports.\n\n==================================================\n1. ARCHITECTURE CONTEXT\n==================================================\n\nThe current intended architecture for myDon Control is:\n\nMain DevPad:\n- work surface\n- terminals\n- seats\n- runtimes\n- identity\n- status\n\nmyDon:\n- Don-facing orchestration surface\n- queue/review\n- handoff routing\n- seat selection\n- later: focus/ping\n- later: VoiceBridge commands\n\nBinding doctrine:\n\nMODEL != SEAT\n\nRouting must resolve by stable seat identity.\n\nDo NOT route by:\n- model\n- provider\n- PID\n- runtime executable\n- launch order\n- window position\n\nThe preferred V1 principle is:\n\nmyDon is a UI + thin API over the EXISTING inbox system.\n\nIt must NOT create a second queue unless the existing inbox genuinely cannot support the needed behavior.\n\n==================================================\n2. CURRENT ARCHITECTURE DECISION TO VERIFY\n==================================================\n\nWe currently have three possible branches.\n\nBRANCH A\nExisting inbox supports:\n- unrouted/pending pool\n- stable item IDs\n- listing/fetching items\n- target assignment or mutation\n\nResult:\nmyDon needs NO separate queue store.\nIt only needs minimal routing/audit residue if the inbox does not already record it.\n\nPreferred outcome if supported.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nBRANCH B\nExisting inbox supports:\n- per-seat inboxes\n- stable items\n- mutation or movement\nBUT:\n- no unrouted/pending pool\n\nResult:\nAdd the smallest unrouted holding lane INSIDE the existing inbox system.\n\nImportant:\nThat holding lane is NOT a seat.\nIt is non-routable.\nIt only holds items awaiting Don.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nBRANCH C\nExisting inbox is effectively:\n- insert-only\n- no stable IDs or no mutation\n- no unrouted pool\n- insufficient status/state support\n\nResult:\nmyDon may need a minimal fallback state file.\n\nEven in Branch C:\ndo not create a large event-sourcing subsystem unless truly necessary.\n\nUse the smallest persistence layer compatible with these invariants:\n- deterministic handoff IDs\n- idempotent delivery\n- explicit failures\n- restart survival\n- auditability\n\n==================================================\n3. PRIMARY RECON TARGETS\n==================================================\n\nInspect all current implementation relevant to:\n\n- command-center inbox MCP\n- ai-inbox\n- queue files\n- queue modules\n- handoff files\n- MCP server code\n- inbox readers/writers\n- any per-seat inbox directories\n- any unrouted/pending location\n- any status fields\n- any IDs\n- any audit/log files\n- any existing routing logic\n- any existing \"ack\", \"done\", \"held\", \"pending\", \"routed\", \"failed\" concepts\n- any DevPad inbox UI elements\n- any seat-inbox mapping\n- any APIs or commands used by current seats to read/write inbox content\n\nLikely canonical root:\n\nC:\\Users\\Dekan AI Brother\\GCE_DON\\command-center\\\n\nKnown relevant areas may include:\n\ninbox-mcp\\\nai-inbox\\\nqueue.js\nqueue.md\nmcp.mjs\nserver.js\nconfig.json\nadapter.js\nadapters\\\ndata\\\n\nDo not assume this list is complete.\n\nSearch current implementation truth.\n\n==================================================\n4. EIGHT REQUIRED RECON QUESTIONS\n==================================================\n\nAnswer all eight with evidence.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nR1 — STABLE IDS / IDEMPOTENCY\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nDo inbox items have stable unique IDs?\n\nDetermine:\n\n- Is an ID generated automatically?\n- Can the writer supply an ID?\n- Is write behavior insert-only?\n- Is there upsert-by-ID?\n- Can an item be fetched by ID?\n- Can duplicates be detected safely?\n\nReturn one of:\n\nSUPPORTED\nPARTIAL\nNOT SUPPORTED\nUNKNOWN\n\nState exact evidence.\n\nThis determines whether inbox itself can enforce idempotency or whether myDon needs a small guard.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nR2 — UNROUTED / PENDING ITEMS\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nCan an item exist inside the inbox system WITHOUT a target seat?\n\nThis is the most important question.\n\nDetermine whether the current system supports:\n\n- unrouted items\n- pending pool\n- central queue\n- holding lane\n- general inbox\n- no-target state\n\nOR whether every write requires a target seat.\n\nReturn:\n\nYES — NATIVE UNROUTED POOL\nYES — CAN REPRESENT UNROUTED ANOTHER WAY\nNO — TARGET REQUIRED\nUNKNOWN\n\nIf yes:\nidentify exact path/schema/API.\n\nThis largely determines Branch A vs B/C.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nR3 — ASSIGNMENT / MOVE / MUTATION\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nCan an existing item be routed after creation?\n\nDetermine whether a current item can have:\n\n- targetSeat changed\n- destination changed\n- inbox moved\n- status changed\n- ownership assigned\n\nOr whether items are immutable after creation.\n\nClassify route mechanics as:\n\nASSIGN-IN-PLACE\nMOVE\nCOPY-WITH-ID\nINSERT-ONLY\nUNKNOWN\n\nPrefer direct observed behavior over intended docs.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nR4 — STATUS / LIFECYCLE SUPPORT\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nWhat lifecycle/status concepts already exist?\n\nSearch for:\n\n- pending\n- queued\n- routed\n- sent\n- held\n- failed\n- acknowledged\n- done\n- completed\n- read/unread\n- processed\n\nDetermine:\n\n- which are persisted\n- which are writable\n- which are UI-only\n- whether arbitrary tags/flags exist\n- whether status mutation exists\n\nMap current capabilities against desired myDon lifecycle:\n\nQUEUED\nHELD\nROUTED\nACKNOWLEDGED\nDONE\nFAILED\n\nDo not force-fit.\nReport actual support.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nR5 — QUERY / LIST CAPABILITIES\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nCan callers:\n\n- list all unrouted items\n- list items by seat\n- fetch one by ID\n- filter by status\n- filter by project\n- filter by priority\n- inspect queue depth\n- inspect unread count\n\nIdentify:\n\n- current APIs\n- MCP methods\n- file readers\n- helper functions\n\nIf current implementation is file-based, explain how these queries work today.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nR6 — PAYLOAD / FIELD CAPABILITIES\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nInventory the actual item schema.\n\nDetermine whether items currently support:\n\n- ID\n- subject/title\n- body/context\n- source\n- target seat\n- project\n- workstream\n- priority\n- status\n- createdAt\n- updatedAt\n- routedAt\n- attachments\n- path references\n- URLs\n- tags\n- metadata\n- audit fields\n\nIdentify size limits if enforced.\n\nImportant:\nWe need a field-mapping table from desired myDon handoff fields to current inbox fields.\n\nDesired logical fields:\n\nhandoffId\nsource\nsubject\ncontext\nproject\nworkstream\npriority\nsuggestedTargetSeat\ntargetSeat\nstatus\nattachments-by-reference\ncreatedAt\nroutedAt\nroutedBy\n\nDo not