omation 2026", "Searching Proton Mail Bridge rate limits automated email terms 2026"], "request_id": "a1a87280-9ac2-4404-bd14-b6e762fd7b3b", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_model_queries": {"queries": ["Proton Mail Bridge IMAP SMTP local client automation 2026", "Proton Mail Bridge rate limits automated email terms 2026"], "type": "search_model_queries"}, "thinking_effort": "standard", "tool_icons": ["globe"], "turn_exchange_id": "f9c9662b-74f6-495c-bf21-1277462fa221", "working_turn_id": "f9c9662b-74f6-495c-bf21-1277462fa221"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": null, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {"real_author": "tool:web"}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Yes — **two-way Pigeon changes the shape**, and email suddenly becomes a very credible transport.\n\nThe cleanest version may actually be:\n\n**Pigeon outbound + Pigeon inbound over a dedicated mailbox.**\n\nThat gives us natural segmentation without cluttering the main Drive estate.\n\n### Why email fits surprisingly well\n\nWith Proton Bridge, the local machine exposes **local IMAP + SMTP servers** that applications on that machine can use; Bridge handles the encryption/decryption behind the scenes. That means a local Pigeon service could potentially **read incoming handoffs via IMAP and send return handoffs via SMTP** without using a public mail API. citeturn222440search0turn222440search2\n\nSo conceptually:\n\n```text\nCoda / Don\n    ↓\nPigeon SEND\n    ↓\ndedicated mailbox\n    ↓\nPigeon RECEIVE\n    ↓\nseat routing / inbox\n    ↓\ntarget does work\n    ↓\nreturn handoff\n    ↓\nPigeon SEND\n    ↓\ndedicated mailbox\n    ↓\nCoda / Don\n```\n\nThat is beautifully isolated.\n\n## I would test **email vs Drive** tomorrow, not choose tonight\n\n### Email lane\nBest properties:\n\n- naturally bidirectional\n- naturally timestamped\n- inherently queued\n- easy SENT / RECEIVED semantics\n- threads can represent a work item\n- attachments available\n- isolated mailbox keeps GCE traffic away from personal mail\n- archiving is already a first-class email concept\n\nThe downside is parsing/threading discipline. We would need a strict machine envelope in the subject/body, e.g.:\n\n```text\n[GCE][HDO-0041][TO:NICO][PROJECT:DEVPAD]\n```\n\nwith structured metadata in the body or attachment.\n\n### Drive lane\nBest properties:\n\n- excellent for **large artifacts**\n- directories provide obvious project segregation\n- file state is inspectable\n- easy archival structure\n- better for long reports/code/specs than email bodies\n\nBut if every tiny handoff becomes another Drive file, I agree: it eventually becomes **filesystem litter** unless Raven/Magpie/cleanup automation aggressively manages it.\n\n## The hybrid may be the winner\n\nThis is the version I’d pressure-test hardest:\n\n**Email = transport/envelope**  \n**Drive/local filesystem = payload/artifact storage**\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nEMAIL\nHandoff HDO-0041\nFrom: Coda\nTo: Nico\nProject: DevPad\n\nObjective:\nImplement runtime binding correction.\n\nArtifact:\nC:\\...\\handoffs\\HDO-0041.md\nor Drive reference\n\nReturn:\nReply with same HDO ID.\n```\n\nNow Pigeon only moves lightweight envelopes while big payloads stay where they belong.\n\n### Two-way becomes very clean\n\nAn outgoing handoff gets:\n\n**HDO-0041**\n\nThe receiving seat returns:\n\n**RE: HDO-0041**\n\nPigeon can correlate that deterministically and route the response back to Coda/Don.\n\nThat gives us something Drive by itself doesn't naturally give us: **a conversation-oriented transport protocol**.\n\n## Proton risk\n\nI would test gently rather than assume automation is unlimited. Proton explicitly says Mail is **not intended for bulk mailing**. Free accounts have published limits of 50 messages/hour and 150/day; paid-plan limits vary with account reputation. Inbound mail is not subject to those sending limits. citeturn222440search8\n\nOur GCE use case would presumably be nowhere near bulk-mail volumes if it's human-scale handoff traffic, but I'd still architect it as:\n\n**low-volume operational messaging, not machine spam.**\n\nAlso, Proton officially supports Bridge with conventional clients and says other IMAP/SMTP clients may work but may not receive the same support. citeturn222440search1 So tomorrow we should prove our automation against the local Bridge rather than assume custom-client behavior.\n\n## The segregation model I really like\n\nGive Pigeon its **own mailbox**.\n\nNot Don's personal inbox.\n\nThen maybe:\n\n- `INBOX` = inbound to GCE\n- `Sent` = outbound GCE\n- `Archive` = completed\n- labels/folders by project if useful\n- nightly cleanup/archive\n- Raven/Magpie can create durable records only for things worth preserving\n\nThen myDon doesn't even have to expose raw email semantics. It can still show:\n\n**NEW HANDOFF → Nico → SEND**\n\nUnderneath, Pigeon happens to use email.\n\nSo yes — **isolation is a feature here**, not a downside.\n\nTomorrow I’d test exactly three candidates:\n\n**A. Proton Bridge bidirectional mail transport**  \n**B. Google Drive transport**  \n**C. Hybrid: email envelope + Drive/local artifact payload**\n\nMy suspicion right now is **C wins**, while **A may be the slickest pure transport lane** if Bridge behaves reliably."]}, "create_time": 1790229707.119907, "end_turn": true, "id": "6c6c6c56-c3be-4bb3-baf3-48924118aa23", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "code_blocks": {"0": {"edited": false, "id": "d4yo4a", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "1": {"edited": false, "id": "hxxd5w", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "2": {"edited": false, "id": "sqt6vq", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}}, "content_references": [{"alt": "([Proton](https://proton.me/support/why-you-need-bridge?utm_source=chatgpt.com))", "end_idx": 694, "error": null, "fallback_items": null, "items": [{"attr