n_shared\\\n```\n\nCurrent route model:\n\n```text\n_shared\\<handoff-id>.json\n\n        ↓ Don chooses seat\n\n<target-seat>\\<same-handoff-id>.json\n\n        ↓ seat consumes/completes\n\n_archive\\\n```\n\n### Frozen branch\n\n```text\nBRANCH A — existing inbox is canonical queue\nVariant: WRITE-ROUTE / MOVE-WITH-ID\n```\n\nThere is **no native assign-in-place MCP operation**.\n\nTherefore routing is a controlled move preserving the canonical handoff identity.\n\n### Canonical ID\n\nThe **filename is authoritative for routing identity**, because live evidence showed that some internal JSON `id` fields do not match filenames.\n\nSo:\n\n```text\ncanonical handoff identity = filename\n```\n\nnot blindly:\n\n```text\njson.id\n```\n\n### Route authorization\n\nTarget directories do **not** define routable seats.\n\nThe allowed targets must come from:\n\n```text\nSEAT REGISTRY\n```\n\nNever:\n\n```text\nfs.readdir(mailboxes)\n```\n\nThis prevents stale/legacy/accidentally-created inbox directories from acquiring routing authority.\n\n---\n\n# 6. myDon persistence — minimal residue only\n\nmyDon does **not** own another queue.\n\nIt persists only what the inbox itself cannot prove.\n\nPotential residue:\n\n```text\nhandoff-id\nfrom-location\ntarget-seat\nrouted-by\nrouted-at\ndelivery-result\nfocus-result\nerror\n```\n\nNothing more unless implementation reality requires it.\n\nHard invariants:\n\n```text\n1. Deterministic handoff identity.\n2. Same handoff → same seat = idempotent/no duplicate.\n3. Different target after route = reject, never silently reroute.\n4. Delivery failure stays visible.\n5. Retry is explicit.\n6. State survives restart.\n7. Routing contains no model/provider/PID authority.\n8. Delivery success does not depend on UI/runtime/focus being alive.\n```\n\n---\n\n# 7. myDon build sequence\n\n## Phase 0 — COMPLETE\n\nInbox recon.\n\nWe now know:\n\n```text\n_shared is the queue\nfilename is canonical ID\nroute = move-with-ID\nseat registry is target authority\nmyDon needs only minimal residue\n```\n\n## Phase 1 — Queue + Route\n\nBuild `/mydon`.\n\nAcceptance test:\n\n```text\n_shared\\HDO-X.json exists\n        ↓\nDon sees HDO-X in /mydon\n        ↓\nDon clicks Luce / presses 6\n        ↓\n_shared\\HDO-X.json disappears\n        ↓\nluce\\HDO-X.json exists exactly once\n        ↓\nmyDon says ROUTED\n        ↓\nnext pending item selected\n```\n\nThen verify:\n\n- server restart retains truth\n- repeat route to Luce creates no duplicate\n- attempted reroute elsewhere is rejected\n- failed move remains visible/retryable\n\nNothing else is required for Phase 1.\n\n## Phase 2 — Route + Focus\n\nOnly after Phase 1 passes.\n\nFocus means:\n\n```text\nfocus/highlight the Luce tile INSIDE DevPad\n```\n\nNot:\n\n```text\nfight Windows HWND behavior\nraise random external windows\ninject terminal keystrokes\n```\n\n## Phase 3 — Ping / ACK / status\n\n- pending badge\n- inbox refresh\n- acknowledgement\n- DONE lifecycle\n\n## Phase 4 — VoiceBridge\n\nVoice commands reuse exactly the same myDon API.\n\n---\n\n# 8. Cable Car + Pigeon — the missing Coda bridge\n\nThis is the other major next problem.\n\nCoda may remain outside the main DevPad.\n\nThat's okay **if the transport makes location irrelevant**.\n\nThe target experience is:\n\n```text\nDon talks to Coda\n      ↓\nCoda produces structured handoff\n      ↓\nCABLE CAR / transport path\n      ↓\nPIGEON\n      ↓\ncurrent Command Center inbox\n      ↓\nmyDon presents/routs it\n      ↓\ntarget seat\n```\n\nAnd critically it must work **both ways**:\n\n```text\nTARGET SEAT\n    ↓\nreturn/result handoff\n    ↓\nPigeon / Cable Car\n    ↓\nCoda + Don\n```\n\nSo Coda no longer requires Don to act as the clipboard.\n\n---\n\n# 9. Working distinction: Cable Car vs Pigeon\n\nKeep these separate.\n\n### Pigeon\n\n**Transport seat / courier behavior.**\n\nPigeon should:\n\n- receive an already-addressed envelope\n- validate basic envelope structure\n- transport it\n- confirm delivery