, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Yes — that maps *really* well to the mixer/orchestration idea.\n\nWhat you’re describing is basically a **gesture control layer** over myDon: finger motion becomes control intent.\n\nExamples:\n\n- **up/down** → raise/lower priority or audio level\n- **left/right** → move between channels/seats\n- **circle clockwise** → increase / advance / zoom into a lane\n- **circle counterclockwise** → decrease / back out\n- **pinch** → select/focus\n- **tap/cue gesture** → send / trigger / acknowledge\n- **hold** → park/hold\n- **open hand** → global overview / stop interception\n- **two-finger swipe** → next handoff / previous handoff\n\nThe smart architecture is the same one we already landed on:\n\n**gesture device → Python/MIDI/OSC adapter → myDon API → seat/audio action**\n\nNot:\n\n**gesture device → direct terminal hacks**\n\nThat keeps the glove/controller completely replaceable. Today it could be a glove, tomorrow a MIDI controller, Leap Motion, camera hand tracking, ring controller, Stream Deck, whatever.\n\nAnd yes, Python is perfect for the adapter layer because a ton of this hardware exposes either **MIDI, HID, OSC, Bluetooth, serial, or SDK events** that Python can normalize.\n\nThe really elegant version is one common control schema:\n\n```text\nFOCUS seat=luce\nROUTE target=nico\nPRIORITY seat=talon value=0.8\nAUDIO music gain=0.25\nDUCK music trigger=voicebridge\nNEXT handoff\nHOLD handoff=current\n```\n\nThen the glove just emits those same commands.\n\nSo the physical controller becomes another **input surface** for the same orchestration API, alongside:\n\n**mouse / keyboard / VoiceBridge / MIDI / gesture / future hardware**\n\nThat’s worth anchoring because it means the mixer idea is not just visual anymore — it can become **embodied control**."]}, "create_time": 1790230096.917971, "end_turn": true, "id": "6b5b41