5d1ed8-8dd4-4cb7-a56b-5f50b6bbe3ca"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": null, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content": "Worked for a couple of seconds", "content_type": "reasoning_recap"}, "create_time": 1790230098.460766, "end_turn": false, "id": "2a4bdb68-9abd-419b-95c5-cc163f824748", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": false, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finished_duration_sec": 1, "hide_inline_actions": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "6cbbfa2e-b63e-48f4-b1ea-6e4f887fddb8", "reasoning_end_time": 1790230098.4550383, "reasoning_recap_type": "hide_all", "reasoning_start_time": 1790230096.968021, "reasoning_status": "reasoning_ended", "request_id": "41567a85-c0f4-41f6-8833-5787cd160a0c", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "b95d1ed8-8dd4-4cb7-a56b-5f50b6bbe3ca", "working_turn_id": "b95d1ed8-8dd4-4cb7-a56b-5f50b6bbe3ca"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": null, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "user"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Here, you're gonna love this. This is the actual detail I was working on separately to fold into this.\n\n\nAbsolutely. Here’s the deep handoff specifically for the Don Orchestrated DevPad control-surface lane — mixer board, audio ducking, DJ-style faders/channels, and wearable/gesture control.\n\nGCE DON ORCHESTRATED DEVPAD — CONTROL SURFACE / MIXER / GESTURE HANDOFF\n\nDATE: 2026-09-23\nAUTHORITY: Don\nSEAT: Coda — Strategic Advisor / Governance\nWORKSTREAM: GCE Stack → Don Orchestrated DevPad → Physical / Gesture Control Surface\nSTATUS: CONCEPT MATURE ENOUGH FOR DEDICATED DESIGN LANE\nCONFIDENCE: 97%\n\n⸻\n\n1. PURPOSE\n\nThis handoff preserves the conceptual state around turning the Don Orchestrated DevPad into more than a screen-based command center.\n\nThe idea is to give Don a physical orchestration layer capable of controlling:\n\n* music volume;\n* VoiceBridge interaction;\n* audio ducking;\n* seat focus;\n* routing;\n* cueing;\n* mode/state changes;\n* potentially individual AI/seat channels;\n* eventually hand/finger gesture control.\n\nThe concept came from Don’s DJ/control-board intuition:\n\nTreat orchestration more like working a mixer than clicking through software.\n\nThe goal is not to build a DJ rig for its own sake.\n\nThe goal is to exploit mature DJ/MIDI/gesture-control hardware as an input surface for GCE orchestration.\n\n⸻\n\n2. CURRENT GCE CONTEXT\n\nThe current Main DevPad is the working command-center surface.\n\nVerified/current layout:\n\nCoda      | Chiave      | Vittorio\nNico      | VoiceBridge | Luce\nTalon     | Keel        | Falcon\n\nCore identity invariant:\n\nTILE != SEAT != MODEL != RUNTIME != PROVIDER\n\nand especially:\n\nMODEL != SEAT\n\nThe DevPad is the work surface.\n\nThe separate higher-level orchestration concept has been called:\n\n* myDon Control\n* Don Controller\n* now more naturally Don Orchestrated DevPad\n\nThe orchestration layer sits above individual panes/seats.\n\nIt should control the existing system rather than replace seat authority or create another competing routing architecture.\n\n⸻\n\n3. RELATED DEVPAD STRUCTURE\n\nA two-pad operating concept has emerged.\n\nMain DevPad\n\nStable project/canonical work.\n\nPurpose:\n\n* durable project sessions;\n* stable seats;\n* current authoritative work;\n* command-center operation.\n\nRookie DevPad\n\nBackup / ad hoc / experimental surface.\n\nPurpose:\n\n* scratch work;\n* one-offs;\n* temporary model auditions;\n* experimental sessions;\n* things that should not contaminate the Main DevPad.\n\nThe physical orchestration concept should eventually be able to address both, but that convergence has not been designed yet.\n\nDo not assume one physical control necessarily maps permanently to one pad.\n\n⸻\n\n4. THE MIXER IDEA\n\nDon proposed using something conceptually similar to a DJ mixer/control board.\n\nThe important insight was not “DJ equipment.”\n\nIt was the interaction model.\n\nA mixer provides:\n\n* multiple channels;\n* dedicated physical controls;\n* relative adjustment rather than binary commands;\n* fast changes without leaving the primary task;\n* tactile state;\n* muscle memory;\n* faders;\n* knobs;\n* cue buttons;\n* mutes;\n* channel selection;\n* crossfading / blending.\n\nThose primitives translate surprisingly well into orchestration.\n\nConceptually:\n\nPHYSICAL CONTROL\n      ↓\nnormalized control event\n      ↓\nDon orchestration layer\n      ↓\nbounded target/action\n      ↓\nDevPad / VoiceBridge / Windows / audio / seat\n\n⸻\n\n5. AUDIO DUCKING — ORIGINAL HIGH-VALUE USE CASE\n\nThe first concrete use case was simple and immediately useful:\n\nDon is working with music playing. He invokes VoiceBridge. Music automatically ducks. VoiceBridge interaction occurs. Music returns afterward.\n\nConcept:\n\nNormal state:\nMusic = normal volume\nDon invokes VoiceBridge:\nMusic ↓\nVoiceBridge active\nVoice interaction ends:\nMusic ↑ smoothly to prior level\n\nThis was enthusiastically accepted because it removes a constant ergonomic interruption.\n\nThe broader Voice Command Plane discussed previously was:\n\nVOICE\n  ↓\nINTERPRETER\n  ↓\nROUTER\n  ↓\nDETERMINISTIC EXECUTOR\n  ↓\nWINDOWS / GCE\n\nThe preferred control hierarchy remains:\n\nAPI / CLI\n>\nprocess/window control\n>\nkeyboard automation\n>\nmouse automation\n\nDucking should eventually use deterministic audio/session control rather than fake GUI clicking where possible.\n\n⸻\n\n6. MIXER CHANNEL CONCEPT\n\nNo permanent channel mapping has been ratified yet.\n\nThe concept is that physical channels could represent things such as:\n\nChannel 1 — Music\nChannel 2 — VoiceBridge\nChannel 3 — Main DevPad\nChannel 4 — Rookie DevPad\nChannel 5 — system / notifications\nChannel 6+ — contextual seat or runtime targets\n\nOr the same hardware could operate contextually rather than assigning every fader permanently.\n\nThe important rule:\n\nA physical channel is not inherently a seat.\n\nA fader is simply an input source.\n\nIts meaning comes from current orchestration context.\n\nThis avoids violating stable-seat doctrine by accidentally making hardware layout authoritative.\n\n⸻\n\n7. POTENTIAL PHYSICAL CONTROL SEMANTICS\n\nThese are conceptual mappings, not yet implementation authority.\n\nA mixer gives useful primitives:\n\nFader\n\nPossible meanings:\n\n* volume;\n* intensity;\n* priority;\n* context allocation;\n* resource weighting;\n* degree of engagement.\n\nRotary encoder / knob\n\nPossible meanings:\n\n* scroll;\n* model/runtime selection;\n* context-window navigation;\n* parameter selection;\n* zoom/detail level;\n* volume or gain.\n\nCue button\n\nHighly interesting for GCE.\n\nPossible meaning:\n\n“Prepare this target without making it active yet.”\n\nExample:\n\nCUE Chiave\n→ prepare/focus architecture lane\n→ no execution yet\nGO / activate\n→ route current object to Chiave\n\nMute\n\nCould mean:\n\n* silence audio channel;\n* suppress notifications;\n* temporarily disable input from a surface;\n* suspend a lane without destroying it.\n\nCrossfader\n\nPotentially useful for:\n\n* smoothly changing attention between two work surfaces;\n* transitioning audio;\n* shifting active orchestration context.\n\nNo specific orchestration meaning is ratified.\n\n⸻\n\n8. IMPORTANT CONTROL DOCTRINE\n\nThe hardware must never become authority.\n\nThe prior gesture doctrine was explicitly accepted:\n\nGesture ≠ device. Device ≠ command. Command ≠ authority.\n\nThis is binding for the design lane.\n\nThe same principle applies to a mixer:\n\nFader ≠ command. Mixer ≠ orchestration authority.\n\nThe physical layer should produce normalized signals.\n\nThen GCE decides what those signals mean within the active context and authority boundaries.\n\nArchitecture:\n\nphysical gesture / control\n        ↓\nnormalized event\n        ↓\nintent interpretation\n        ↓\ncontext resolution\n        ↓\nauthority check\n        ↓\nbounded deterministic action\n\n⸻\n\n9. THE HAND / RING / GLOVE IDEA\n\nDon extended the mixer concept beyond a physical board.\n\nThe idea:\n\nUse hand movement itself as a controller.\n\nExamples Don explicitly imagined included:\n\n* finger up;\n* finger down;\n* moving around;\n* circular movement;\n* cueing;\n* potentially controlling Python/GCE with gestures.\n\nThis should not be tied to one specific wearable.\n\nThe durable architecture is gesture-first, device-second.\n\n⸻\n\n10. PRIOR GESTURE HARDWARE REFERENCES\n\nSeveral candidate technologies were previously identified as references or possible test hardware.\n\nThese were not selected purchases or architecture dependencies.\n\nGenki Wave\n\nFinger-worn / ring-like MIDI control device.\n\nInteresting because it demonstrates:\n\n* wrist/finger motion as continuous input;\n* gesture → MIDI/control signal;\n* wearable operation without requiring a camera.\n\nPreviously discussed approximate price was around $349, but that is historical reference only and should not be treated as a current shopping quote.\n\nEnhancia Neova\n\nRing-based musical gesture-control reference.\n\nRelevant historically because it showed that expressive hand motion can be translated into control data.\n\nMiMU Gloves / Glover\n\nStrong conceptual reference because MiMU treats body/hand movement as a programmable musical control interface.\n\nPotential output styles include:\n\n* MIDI;\n* OSC;\n* mapped gesture events.\n\nTornado A1 / other glove-style devices\n\nReferenced as possible wearable gesture-control examples.\n\nNo specific product was selected.\n\nCamera + MediaPipe\n\nImportant non-wearable alternative.\n\nCamera sees:\n\n* hand pose;\n* finger position;\n* gesture;\n* direction;\n* movement.\n\nThis could be useful as a proving-ground implementation before specialized hardware.\n\n⸻\n\n11. GESTURE NORMALIZATION MODEL\n\nA device should not directly emit things like:\n\n\"TURN MUSIC DOWN\"\n\nInstead, it should emit normalized physical state.\n\nExample:\n\n{\n  \"motion\": \"rotate\",\n  \"direction\": \"clockwise\",\n  \"amplitude\": 0.42,\n  \"velocity\": 0.31,\n  \"duration_ms\": 480,\n  \"confidence\": 0.96,\n  \"context\": \"right_hand\"\n}\n\nUseful normalized dimensions previously discussed:\n\n* motion type;\n* direction;\n* amplitude;\n* duration;\n* velocity;\n* confidence;\n* context.\n\nThen an interpreter can map:\n\nclockwise rotation\n+\ncurrent context = music\n→ raise volume\n\nor:\n\nclockwise rotation\n+\ncurrent context = seat selection\n→ move selection right\n\nSame gesture. Different context. No hardware lock-in.\n\n⸻\n\n12. WHY THIS MATTERS\n\nThe strongest conceptual insight is:\n\nReverse-engineer the interface, not the gadget.\n\nDon should not build the GCE around some specific ring, glove, MIDI controller, or mixer.\n\nInstead define:\n\nCONTROL EVENT CONTRACT\n\nThen allow many devices to satisfy it.\n\nPossible future inputs:\n\nDJ mixer\nMIDI controller\nmacro pad\nring\nglove\ncamera\ntouchscreen\nmouse\nkeyboard\nvoice\nspatial controller\nphone\n\nAll can feed the same orchestration layer.\n\n⸻\n\n13. MACRO DEVICES / SIMPLE CONTROLLERS\n\nA macro keyboard or MIDI controller may be valuable as a test harness.\n\nBut it should not become the architecture.\n\nExample:\n\nbutton press\n→ normalized \"CUE\" event\n\nLater:\n\nfinger point\n→ same normalized \"CUE\" event\n\nIf both work through the same event contract, the architecture is healthy.\n\n⸻\n\n14. DON ORCHESTRATED DEVPAD — CONCEPTUAL STACK\n\nThe current design direction can be summarized as:\n\n                    DON\n                     │\n       ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐\n       │                           │\n   PHYSICAL                     VOICE\n   CONTROL                     CONTROL\n       │                           │\n       ├─ mixer                    ├─ VoiceBridge\n       ├─ faders                   └─ spoken intent\n       ├─ knobs\n       ├─ buttons\n       ├─ ring\n       ├─ glove\n       └─ camera gestures\n               │\n               ▼\n       NORMALIZED EVENT BUS\n               │\n               ▼\n          DON CONTROLLER\n               │\n       context + authority\n               │\n               ▼\n       deterministic adapters\n               │\n      ┌────────┼─────────┐\n      ▼        ▼         ▼\n   DevPad   VoiceBridge  Windows/audio\n\nThis is conceptual, not yet a committed implementation architecture.\n\n⸻\n\n15. AUDIO AS A FIRST PROVING GROUND\n\nAudio ducking is probably one of the strongest first primitives because the behavior is easy to observe.\n\nPotential first experiment:\n\nmusic playing\n    ↓\nphysical control / VoiceBridge activation\n    ↓\nmusic session reduced\n    ↓\nVoiceBridge active\n    ↓\ninteraction completes\n    ↓\nmusic restored to previous value\n\nThe important technical requirement:\n\nrestore previous state, not some hard-coded volume.\n\nExample:\n\nbefore = 73%\nduck = 20%\nrestore = 73%\n\nnot:\n\nduck = 20%\nrestore = 80%\n\nState must survive the interaction.\n\n⸻\n\n16. POTENTIAL MIXER/DJ CONTROL TRANSPORT\n\nThe design lane should investigate standard control protocols before inventing anything.\n\nEspecially:\n\nMIDI\n\nAttractive because:\n\n* enormous hardware ecosystem;\n* sliders/knobs/buttons are naturally represented;\n* mature Python libraries exist;\n* low latency;\n* controllers often expose values deterministically.\n\nOSC\n\nAttractive for:\n\n* networked control;\n* richer addressable event structures;\n* gesture applications;\n* MiMU-style ecosystems.\n\nPotential long-term abstraction:\n\nMIDI\nOSC\ngesture SDK\ncamera vision\nkeyboard\ntouch\n        ↓\nGCE Control Adapter\n        ↓\nnormalized event\n\nNo transport is yet authoritative.\n\n⸻\n\n17. RELATIONSHIP TO VOICEBRIDGE\n\nVoiceBridge should not become a separate control architecture.\n\nThe earlier Don Controller / myDon concept explicitly favored:\n\nreuse one orchestration API rather than add parallel logic.\n\nThus:\n\nMixer ──────┐\nGesture ────┤\nTouch ──────┤\nVoiceBridge ┤\n            ▼\n       same control plane\n\nVoiceBridge is one input/output surface.\n\nIt should not own global orchestration semantics.\n\n⸻\n\n18. RELATIONSHIP TO CABLE CAR / PIGEON\n\nSeparate but adjacent concept:\n\nCable Car = bridge / transport path.\nPigeon = two-way courier / confirmation.\n\nPigeon should transport and acknowledge.\n\nPigeon should not interpret orchestration intent.\n\nLikewise the mixer/gesture surface should not bypass routing and directly mutate distant systems.\n\nPossible eventual flow:\n\ngesture\n↓\nDon Controller\n↓\nbounded action request\n↓\nCable Car\n↓\nPigeon transport/confirmation\n↓\nremote/other surface\n\nBut this integration has not been designed or ratified.\n\n⸻\n\n19. WHAT IS NOT YET DECIDED\n\nDo not carry any of the following forward as settled:\n\n* specific DJ mixer;\n* specific MIDI controller;\n* specific wearable ring;\n* specific glove;\n* specific camera stack;\n* exact fader mappings;\n* exact number of channels;\n* whether controls are fixed or contextual;\n* exact MIDI message schema;\n* exact OSC schema;\n* whether Python is the final control daemon;\n* whether hardware talks directly to DevPad;\n* whether physical controls manage seats individually;\n* whether a crossfader has orchestration meaning;\n* exact duck percentage;\n* automatic return timing;\n* gesture vocabulary;\n* gesture confirmation model;\n* purchase decisions.\n\nThese remain design/research territory.\n\n⸻\n\n20. DESIGN INVARIANTS TO PRESERVE\n\nAny future architecture pass should keep these visible:\n\nIDENTITY != ROLE != MODEL\nMODEL != SEAT\nTILE != SEAT != MODEL != RUNTIME != PROVIDER\nGESTURE != DEVICE\nDEVICE != COMMAND\nCOMMAND != AUTHORITY\nINPUT != INTENT\nINTENT != AUTHORITY\nPHYSICAL CONTROL != AUTHORITY\nVOICEBRIDGE != GLOBAL ROUTER\nTRANSPORT != INTERPRETER\n\nAnd:\n\nOne owner per responsibility.\n\n⸻\n\n21. BEST NEXT DESIGN ROUTE\n\nWhen Don deliberately reopens this lane, the correct sequence is probably:\n\n1. Define the normalized control-event contract.\n2. Pick one observable primitive.\n3. Use cheap/simple hardware as proving ground.\n4. Verify deterministic action.\n5. Only then evaluate specialized mixer/ring/glove hardware.\n\nThe natural first primitive already identified is:\n\naudio duck / restore\n\nbecause it exercises:\n\n* continuous control;\n* state capture;\n* deterministic execution;\n* physical/voice trigger;\n* visible result;\n* state restoration.\n\nA basic MIDI fader could prove the entire control plane before any exotic glove is purchased.\n\n⸻\n\n22. LONGER-TERM VISION\n\nThe eventual experience Don is imagining looks less like operating individual apps and more like conducting an environment.\n\nExamples:\n\n* rotate hand → lower music;\n* point/cue → arm a seat;\n* flick → move focus;\n* fader → adjust one continuous system parameter;\n* button → cue VoiceBridge;\n* hand circle → scroll or cycle context;\n* gesture + context → bounded command;\n* voice + gesture together → richer explicit intent.\n\nThe Don Orchestrated DevPad therefore becomes:\n\na multimodal orchestration instrument rather than merely a dashboard.\n\nThe screen remains useful, but Don’s hands, voice, physical controls, and eventually spatial gestures can all become first-class inputs into the same deterministic command plane.\n\n⸻\n\n23. COMPACT HANDOFF TO NEXT CHAT\n\nCURRENT TRUTH:\nDon wants to explore a physical orchestration layer for the Don Orchestrated DevPad using mixer/DJ-style controls, audio ducking, and eventually hand/ring/glove gestures.\n\nPROVEN/ACCEPTED CONCEPTS:\nAudio ducking while working; gesture/device separation; normalized-event architecture; hardware must remain swappable; physical input never becomes authority.\n\nMAIN CONTROL PRINCIPLE:\nphysical signal → normalized event → context/intent → authority check → deterministic action\n\nREFERENCE HARDWARE:\nDJ/MIDI controllers, Genki Wave, Enhancia Neova, MiMU/Glover-style gloves, other gesture gloves, camera + MediaPipe. References only; nothing selected.\n\nFIRST PROVING PRIMITIVE:\nMusic duck/restore around VoiceBridge interaction.\n\nCURRENT DEVPAD CONTEXT:\nMain DevPad stable 3×3; separate Rookie DevPad planned for ad hoc/experimental work; Don Controller/myDon is the orchestration concept above DevPad.\n\nADJACENT TRANSPORT:\nCable Car = bridge; Pigeon = courier/confirmation, not interpreter.\n\nDO NOT DO:\nDo not hard-code hardware identity into command semantics. Do not assign authority to gestures. Do not build parallel VoiceBridge routing. Do not buy/design around one gadget before defining the event contract.\n\nNEXT ROUTE:\nDedicated Don Orchestrated DevPad — Physical Control / Gesture / Audio design chat → define event contract → prove duck/restore using simple control hardware → then evaluate DJ controller vs ring/glove/camera.\n\n⸻\n\nHANDOFF STATUS\n\nSTATUS: READY FOR DEDICATED DESIGN LANE\nAUTHORITY: Don\nIMPLEMENTATION: NOT STARTED\nHARDWARE SELECTION: OPEN\nCORE DOCTRINE: STABLE\nFIRST TEST TARGET: AUDIO DUCK / RESTORE\nFRAMEWORK IMPACT: Candidate lessons only; requires reconciliation before promotion."]}, "create_time": 1790230279.883209, "end_turn": null, "id": "2f4a2ef5-cdc9-435b-88e4-bbb2f516b6a2", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "dictation": true, "dictation_original_text": "Here, you're gonna love this. This is the actual detail I was working on separately to fold into this.", "message_source": null, "request_id": "4835ef40-f064-425a-b879-49507412f1dc", "triggered_by_system_hint_suggestion": false, "turn_exchange_id": "abbdcd05-0021-4d91-8fb4-076713011db5", "working_turn_id": "abbdcd05-0021-4d91-8fb4-076713011db5"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790230300.015862, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["This is excellent. It **fully closes the gap** between the mixer idea and the gesture-control lane.\n\nWhat I’d anchor from this handoff is:\n\n**The control surface is not “DJ hardware.” It is a multimodal input layer.**\n\nThe durable architecture is:\n\n```text\nPHYSICAL / VOICE / GESTURE INPUT\n        ↓\nNORMALIZED CONTROL EVENT\n        ↓\nCONTEXT / INTENT RESOLUTION\n        ↓\nAUTHORITY CHECK\n        ↓\nDETERMINISTIC ACTION\n        ↓\nDEVPAD / VOICEBRIDGE / WINDOWS / AUDIO\n```\n\nAnd the strongest binding doctrine coming out of this is:\n\n```text\nGESTURE != DEVICE\nDEVICE != COMMAND\nCOMMAND != AUTHORITY\n\nPHYSICAL CONTROL != AUTHORITY\nVOICEBRIDGE != GLOBAL ROUTER\nTRANSPORT != INTERPRETER\n```\n\nThat is the right abstraction. It keeps the whole thing from becoming “we bought a cool glove, now architect around it.”\n\n## What this adds to tonight’s anchor\n\nWe now have a clean four-surface picture:\n\n**Main DevPad**  \nstable project cockpit\n\n**Rookie DevPad**  \nad hoc / experimental cockpit\n\n**myDon Control**  \norchestration / routing controller\n\n**Physical / Gesture Control Layer**  \nhands, mixer, faders, rings, gloves, camera, MIDI, OSC, voice\n\nAnd that physical layer should feed the **same myDon control plane**, not create another parallel command system.\n\n## Best first proving ground\n\nThe handoff is right: **audio duck / restore** is almost perfect as the first implementation primitive.\n\nWhy it’s such a strong test:\n\n- stateful\n- continuous\n- reversible\n- easy to observe\n- low authority risk\n- useful immediately\n- works with voice and physical control\n- forces us to preserve previous state correctly\n\nThe acceptance pattern should be:\n\n```text\ncapture current music volume\n→ VoiceBridge active / physical trigger\n→ duck to configured level\n→ interaction ends\n→ restore exact previous volume\n```\n\nNot “restore to some default.”\n\n## What I would test first\n\nBefore buying anything exotic:\n\n1. **MIDI fader / knob**\n2. Python or lightweight adapter\n3. normalized event\n4. Windows audio/session control\n5. duck / restore\n6. then map a second control to something non-audio, like **focus seat 6**\n\nIf both work through the same event contract, the architecture is proven.\n\nThen the hardware audition becomes fun instead of foundational:\n\n- cheap MIDI controller\n- Genki Wave\n- MiMU glove\n- camera + MediaPipe\n- other gesture devices\n\n## The key next artifact\n\nWhen this lane reopens, I would not start with hardware shopping.\n\nI’d start with a tiny **Control Event Contract**, something like:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"source\": \"midi|gesture|voice|touch\",\n  \"control\": \"fader|rotate|tap|cue|swipe\",\n  \"value\": 0.42,\n  \"direction\": \"up|down|left|right|clockwise|counterclockwise\",\n  \"velocity\": 0.31,\n  \"duration_ms\": 480,\n  \"confidence\": 0.96,\n  \"context\": \"music|seat|handoff|global\"\n}\n```\n\nThen the interpreter maps that to:\n\n```text\nAUDIO_DUCK\nAUDIO_RESTORE\nFOCUS_SEAT\nROUTE_HANDOFF\nNEXT_HANDOFF\nHOLD_HANDOFF\nCUE_SEAT\n```\n\nThat is the actual heart of the system.\n\nAnd the most exciting part is that the **same contract can accept a physical fader, a ring, a glove, a camera gesture, or VoiceBridge** without changing the downstream orchestration model.\n\nThat’s the right place to freeze this for tonight."]}, "create_time": 1790230281.102887, "end_turn": true, "id": "7a925880-4b4d-4c3b-80cd-bde3cde9a265", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "code_blocks": {"0": {"edited": false, "id": "yct4wi", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "1": {"edited": false, "id": "mdr170", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "2": {"edited": false, "id