is not a valid file id: 88464138-c1a8-429e-baea-3710ebb6681a", "start_ix": 1121}], "content_references": [{"alt": null, "end_idx": 223, "invalid": true, "matched_text": "fileciteturn0file9", "prefix": null, "prompt_text": null, "refs": [], "safe_urls": [], "start_idx": 202, "type": "hidden"}, {"alt": null, "end_idx": 334, "invalid": true, "matched_text": "fileciteturn0file0", "prefix": null, "prompt_text": null, "refs": [], "safe_urls": [], "start_idx": 313, "type": "hidden"}, {"alt": null, "end_idx": 645, "invalid": true, "matched_text": "fileciteturn0file0", "prefix": null, "prompt_text": null, "refs": [], "safe_urls": [], "start_idx": 624, "type": "hidden"}, {"alt": null, "end_idx": 777, "invalid": true, "matched_text": "fileciteturn0file0", "prefix": null, "prompt_text": null, "refs": [], "safe_urls": [], "start_idx": 756, "type": "hidden"}, {"alt": null, "end_idx": 909, "invalid": true, "matched_text": "fileciteturn0file0", "prefix": null, "prompt_text": null, "refs": [], "safe_urls": [], "start_idx": 888, "type": "hidden"}, {"alt": null, "end_idx": 1034, "invalid": true, "matched_text": "fileciteturn0file9", "prefix": null, "prompt_text": null, "refs": [], "safe_urls": [], "start_idx": 1013, "type": "hidden"}, {"alt": null, "end_idx": 1142, "invalid": true, "matched_text": "fileciteturn0file0", "prefix": null, "prompt_text": null, "refs": [], "safe_urls": [], "start_idx": 1121, "type": "hidden"}, {"alt": null, "end_idx": 1355, "invalid": false, "matched_text": "memcite", "prefix": null, "prompt_text": null, "refs": [], "safe_urls": [], "start_idx": 1346, "type": "hidden"}], "conversation_context_citation_metadata": [{"citation": {"alt": "", "attribution": "Past chat", "category": "memory", "end_idx": 0, "matched_text": "", "prompt_text": null, "pub_date": 1790216903.621, "refs": [], "retrieval_origin": "pca", "snippet": "Bedrock model. Availability for framework understanding.   Yep — that quota dump is the smoking gun, and it is worth preserving exactly as **account-level evidence**:\n\n> `QuotaAppliedAtLevel: ACCOUNT` + `Value: 0.0` + several critical Nova request/token quotas marked `Adjustable: false`.\n\nThat is much stronger than “maybe Nova is having a region issue.” The account can discover Nova, resolve the inference profile, and authenticate — but the classic Bedrock Runtime lane is effectively shut for those Nova inference dimensions.\n\nSo: **leave Nova alone for now.** We have a healthy Mantle estate and $159.26 of runway.\n\n## Bedrock Mantle estate — first-pass cost map\n\nI’m separating **verified-live**, **visible**, and **runtime-blocked** so we don’t accidentally promote inventory visibility into proven execution.\n\n| ModelMantle stateStandard input / 1MStandard output / 1MContextBest GCE use |                      |           |            |                         |                                  |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | --------- | ---------- | ----------------------- | -------------------------------- |\n| **GLM 4.7 Flash**                                                           | Visible              | \\~$0.07   | \\~$0.40    | 203K                    | cheap utility / extraction       |\n| **Nemotron Super 3 120B**                                                   | Visible              | \\~$0.15   | \\~$0.65    | 256K                    | cheap agentic worker             |\n| **GPT-5.6 Luna**                                                            | Visible              | **$0.22** | **$1.32**  | up to 1M family context | fast general worker              |\n| **DeepSeek V3.2**                                                           | Visible              | \\~$0.62   | \\~$1.85    | 164K                    | reasoning / coding               |\n| **GLM 4.7**                                                                 | Visible              | \\~$0.60   | \\~$2.20    | 203K                    | general reasoning                |\n| **Kimi K2 Thinking**                                                        | Visible              | \\~$0.60   | \\~$2.50    | long-context            | deliberate reasoning             |\n| **Grok 4.3**                                                                | Visible              | **$1.25** | **$2.50**  | **1M**                  | agentic reasoning / huge context |\n| **Kimi K2.5**                                                               | **LIVE TEST PASSED** | \\~$0.60   | \\~$3.00    | **256K**                | coding + reasoning default       |\n| **GLM 5**                                                                   | Visible              | \\~$1.00   | \\~$3.20    | **200K**                | systems / architecture           |\n| **GPT-5.6 Sol**                                                             | Visible              | **$4.40** | **$22.00** | **1M**                  | premium escalation only          |\n\nThe AWS docs independently confirm the capability side: Kimi K2.5 is 256K context/16K output; DeepSeek V3.2 is 164K/8K; GLM 5 is 200K; Grok 4.3 is 1M and configurable-reasoning; GPT-5.6 Sol is 1M context. ([AWS Documentation](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/model-card-moonshot-ai-kimi-k2-5.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com \"Kimi K2.5 - Amazon Bedrock\"))\n\nAWS also confirms **GPT-5.6 Luna** is radically cheaper than Sol: $0.22/M input and $1.32/M output for short-context in-region traffic, versus Sol at $4.40/M input and $22/M output. ([AWS Documentation](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/model-card-openai-gpt-56-luna.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com \"GPT-5.6 Luna - Amazon Bedrock\"))\n\n## The immediate GCE economics are excellent\n\nTake a representative **100K-input / 10K-output** job:\n\n- GLM 4.7 Flash: about **$0.011**\n- Nemotron Super: about **$0.0215**\n- GPT-5.6 Luna: about **$0.035**\n- DeepSeek V3.2: about **$0.0805**\n- Kimi K2.5: about **$0.090**\n- GLM 5: about **$0.132**\n- Grok 4.3: about **$0.150**\n- GPT-5.6 Sol: about **$0.66**\n\nThat means your **$159.26** is not emergency scraps. At Kimi K2.5-type usage, it represents a lot of serious work. At GLM Flash/Luna utility workloads, it represents a *huge* amount.\n\n### The architecture that falls out of this\n\nI would not make one Bedrock model do everything.\n\nThe natural economic lanes are:\n\n- **Cheap utility:** `zai.glm-4.7-flash`\n- **Cheap strong worker:** `nvidia.nemotron-super-3-120b`\n- **Fast OpenAI worker:** `openai.gpt-5.6-luna`\n- **Reasoning/coding value:** `deepseek.v3.2`\n- **Primary Kimi lane:** `moonshotai.kimi-k2.5`\n- **Deliberate Kimi reasoning:** `moonshotai.kimi-k2-thinking`\n- **Architecture/heavy systems:** `zai.glm-5`\n- **Huge-context alternative:** `xai.grok-4.3`\n- **Premium exception:** `openai.gpt-5.6-sol`\n\nThat is **capability routing**, not changing seat authority. MODEL ≠ SEAT.\n\n## There is one major endpoint trap we need to document\n\nNot every Mantle model uses the same path.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nKimi / GLM / DeepSeek\nhttps://bedrock-mantle.us-east-2.api.aws/v1\n```\n\nBut AWS documents **Grok 4.3** on:\n\n```text\nhttps://bedrock-mantle.us-east-2.api.aws/openai/v1\n```\n\nand GPT-5.6 Sol also uses the `/openai/v1` family. ([AWS Documentation](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/model-card-xai-grok-4-3.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com \"Grok 4.3 - Amazon Bedrock\"))\n\nThat is exactly the kind of thing that can recreate today’s mess if we build one universal provider configuration and assume every model speaks through the identical base path.\n\n## Next move: make this extraction empirical\n\nBefore we wire anything into GCE, I’d run a **micro-probe matrix** against the 8 models we actually care about.\n\nSame prompt:\n\n```text\nReturn exactly: BEDROCK_OK\n```\n\nSame tiny output.\n\nCapture:\n\n```text\nmodel_id\nendpoint/base-path\nsuccess/failure\nlatency\nprompt_tokens\ncompletion_tokens\nservice_tier\n```\n\nNo more than a few dozen tokens each.\n\nThen our estate changes from:\n\n**AVAILABLE**\n\nto:\n\n**LIVE VERIFIED**\n\nmodel by model.\n\nAfter that, I can build you the final **Bedrock GCE Runtime Matrix** sorted three ways:\n\n**cost → capability → preferred GCE workload**, including exact model IDs, endpoint variants, context limits, cache economics, and fallback order.\n\nAnd the Nova evidence gets quarantined as:\n\n**CLASSIC RUNTIME / ACCOUNT QUOTA GATED — DO NOT TROUBLESHOOT FURTHER UNTIL AWS CHANGES ACCOUNT QUOTAS.**", "start_idx": 0, "title": "Bedrock Framework Understanding", "type": "grouped_webpages", "url": "https://chatgpt.com/c/6ab48ac4-36e4-83e8-83b8-0a5b17a804ca"}, "citation_uuid": "cbcb6f58-82e5-473c-bf8e-71fe1596a05b", "deleted": false, "feedback": null, "retrieval_origin": "pca"}, {"citation": {"alt": "", "attribution": "Past chat", "category": "memory", "end_idx": 0, "matched_text": "", "prompt_text": null, "pub_date": 1790116104.686, "refs": [], "retrieval_origin": "pca", "snippet": "DATE: 2026-09-22\\\nSESSION: New Chat Bootstrap — Kimi Desktop Experimental MLB\\\nPROJECT: EC8 - Edge Crew\\\nSEAT CONTEXT: Coda governance / Don final authority\\\nLANE: Experimental MLB pressure-test\n\n# PURPOSE\n\nCreate a separate conversational lane called:\n\n**Kimi Desktop Experimental MLB**\n\nThis chat exists so Don can use the existing Kimi Desktop / legacy Quant environment for live MLB analysis, paper experimentation, methodology pressure-testing, and fun without contaminating the authoritative Parent Quant build currently frozen in the terminal lane.\n\nThis is deliberately an **experimental sport-specific lane**.\n\nIt is NOT the Main Edge Crew Quant parent thread.\n\nIt is NOT Gate 7 implementation authority.\n\nIt is NOT permission to modify the authoritative terminal build.\n\n# FROZEN AUTHORITATIVE STATE\n\nThe authoritative Parent Quant build lane remains frozen where terminal work stopped.\n\nCurrent authoritative sequence:\n\n**Gate 5 Technical Methodology**\\\n→ FORMALLY ACCEPTED\n\n**Gate 6 Chiave Architecture**\\\n→ ACCEPTED / BINDING ARCHITECTURE LAYER\n\n**Gate 7**\\\n→ AUTHORIZED\n\nLocal project root:\n\n`C:\\Users\\Dekan AI Brother\\GCE_DON\\GCE_EC8\\GCE_EdgeCrew_Quant`\n\nApproved new parent substrate location:\n\n`C:\\Users\\Dekan AI Brother\\GCE_DON\\GCE_EC8\\GCE_EdgeCrew_Quant\\08_Parent_Quant\\`\n\nTerminal Kimi completed the Gate 7 reconciliation and proposed P1–P6 implementation.\n\nDon approved the placement and authorized P1 materialization.\n\nExecution then paused because terminal Kimi credits were exhausted.\n\n**Treat that terminal state as FROZEN.**\n\nDo not infer that experimental work in this chat changes it.\n\n# HARD ISOLATION RULE\n\nNothing produced in this experimental MLB chat automatically becomes:\n\n- Parent Quant methodology\n- Gate 5 canon\n- Gate 6 architecture\n- Gate 7 implementation\n- production authority\n- canonical MLB methodology\n- a modification to the local terminal build\n\nThe experimental lane may discover useful ideas.\n\nThose ideas must travel through:\n\n**OBSERVE**\\\n→ **EXTRACT**\\\n→ **PRESSURE-TEST**\\\n→ **PROPOSE**\\\n→ **PARENT / SPORT RECONCILIATION**\\\n→ **FORMAL ADOPTION IF ACCEPTED**\n\nNo silent promotion.\n\n# WHY THIS CHAT EXISTS\n\nDon wants to have some fun using MLB while the terminal implementation lane is temporarily paused.\n\nThe legacy Kimi Desktop Quant environment contains useful MLB history, workflows, grading behavior, and operating context.\n\nWe may use that estate as an experimental proving ground.\n\nIts legacy behavior does not become authoritative merely because it already exists.\n\n# LEGACY KIMI DESKTOP CONTEXT\n\nKimi Desktop was given the new Parent Quant methodology and related governance/architecture material.\n\nIts first reconciliation response surfaced:\n\n- Adaptation Patch v1.0\n- float-path quarantine\n- named-snapshot edge calculation\n- STALE retirement/display behavior\n- three-view cards\n- Coda judgment → Don Delta handling\n- updated vocabulary\n- Gate 8 validation questions\n- legacy v2.1.x engine disposition\n- Chiave E1–E6 architecture items\n- MLB amendment queue\n- reliability-band/color mapping\n- scale targets\n- Season 1 paper ledger requirements\n- Process v2 legacy/refactor material\n- book + timestamp requirements for market observations\n\nTreat these as **reconciliation findings**, not automatically accepted parent methodology.\n\n# PARENT QUANT RULES THAT STILL GOVERN THE EXPERIMENT\n\nEven in experimental MLB work, preserve the important structural principles.\n\n## Three analytical views\n\n**BASELINE**\\\n→ canonical analytical state\n\n**DON DELTA**\\\n→ separate versioned human overlay\n\n**MODEL SYNTHESIS**\\\n→ separate derived third view\n\nDo not silently mutate Baseline.\n\n## Market observations\n\nEvery line used analytically should identify, when available:\n\n- book/source\n- timestamp\n- market\n- price/line\n- observation/snapshot identity\n\nA pasted line is an observation, not an eternal market truth.\n\n## Evidence discipline\n\nPreserve:\n\n- DIRECT\n- DERIVED\n- PROXY\n- UNAVAILABLE\n\nAnd:\n\n**PROXY != DIRECT**\n\n**MISSING != ZERO**\n\n**DATA PRIORITY != MODEL WEIGHT**\n\n## State discipline\n\nDo not overwrite pregame analysis with later/live information.\n\nHistorical observations should remain point-in-time identifiable.\n\n# EXPERIMENTAL FREEDOM\n\nWithin those boundaries, this lane may move quickly.\n\nWe can:\n\n- run MLB cards\n- compare legacy vs new presentation\n- paper-grade games\n- test Baseline / Don Delta / Synthesis\n- experiment with stale-data presentation\n- inspect model disagreement\n- explore component behavior\n- examine market movement\n- test proposed MLB amendments\n- compare outputs against final results\n- identify useful UX\n- discover failures\n- challenge assumptions\n- generate candidate improvements\n\nThe point is to produce evidence.\n\nWe do NOT need to stop every few minutes to redesign Parent Quant.\n\n# LEGACY ENGINE\n\nThe legacy MLB engine may be used experimentally if clearly labeled.\n\nRecommended label:\n\n**LEGACY / FLOAT-PATH / EXPERIMENTAL**\n\nIts outputs are evidence.\n\nThey are not authoritative Parent Quant outputs.\n\nDo not conceal differences between the legacy engine and the accepted new methodology.\n\nThose differences are useful experimental evidence.\n\n# MLB-SPECIFIC ITEMS STAY MLB-SPECIFIC\n\nExamples include:\n\n- cushion minimums\n- unit/exposure caps\n- pitcher-injury adjustments\n- MLB reliability bands\n- MLB component definitions\n- MLB weights\n- pitcher/bullpen/lineup variables\n- MLB-specific live-state behavior\n\nThese belong to the MLB module lane unless a reusable principle is later extracted and reconciled cross-sport.\n\nDo not promote them into Parent Quant merely because they work for baseball.\n\n# GATE 8\n\nDo not invent universal Gate 8 numeric minimums in this experimental chat.\n\nWe may collect:\n\n- replay counts\n- paper-trade counts\n- calibration observations\n- failure examples\n- shadow-run evidence\n\nThat evidence can later inform formal QA / Validation methodology.\n\n# EXPERIMENTAL OUTPUT DISCIPLINE\n\nWhen something interesting is discovered, classify it as one of:\n\n**MLB ONLY**\\\nSport-specific finding.\n\n**CROSS-SPORT CANDIDATE**\\\nMay represent a reusable Quant principle but has not been adopted.\n\n**UX / PRODUCT CANDIDATE**\\\nPresentation or human-interaction improvement.\n\n**DATA FINDING**\\\nSource, freshness, provenance, coverage, or evidence issue.\n\n**ENGINEERING FINDING**\\\nImplementation behavior or technical constraint.\n\n**PARENT GOVERNANCE CANDIDATE**\\\nPotential change requiring Coda reconciliation and Don ruling.\n\nThis classification does not itself promote anything.\n\n# DON DELTA\n\nDon may give subjective baseball judgments, situational reads, contextual observations, or disagreement with the baseline.\n\nPreserve these as Don input.\n\nClassify/validate them where appropriate.\n\nDo not silently convert Don's opinion into Baseline.\n\nUse the lifecycle:\n\n**BASELINE**\\\n→ **STATUS SUMMARY**\\\n→ **DASHBOARD**\\\n→ **DRILL-DOWN**\\\n→ **DON INPUT**\\\n→ **CLASSIFY / VALIDATE**\\\n→ **DON DELTA**\\\n→ **MODEL SYNTHESIS**\\\n→ **REANALYZE**\n\n# OPERATING STYLE\n\nThis lane is intentionally conversational and fast.\n\nWe are allowed to enjoy baseball.\n\nDo not burden Don with unnecessary architecture ceremony during ordinary game analysis.\n\nOnly surface framework issues when the experiment reveals something genuinely useful.\n\n# TERMINAL FIREWALL\n\nDo not:\n\n- modify `08_Parent_Quant`\n- modify Gate 5 artifacts\n- modify Gate 6 artifacts\n- claim Gate 7 completion\n- create canonical parent files\n- instruct terminal Kimi to absorb experimental findings\n- treat this chat as production authority\n\nuntil Don explicitly initiates reconciliation.\n\n# WHEN WE RETURN TO THE FORMAL BUILD\n\nAt a later checkpoint, Coda and Don may review the experimental evidence and produce a compact extraction:\n\n**What happened**\\\n→ **What worked**\\\n→ **What failed**\\\n→ **What is MLB-specific**\\\n→ **What may generalize**\\\n→ **What should be tested elsewhere**\\\n→ **What, if anything, should enter formal methodology**\n\nUntil then, experimental findings remain experimental.\n\n# CURRENT STATUS\n\n**AUTHORITATIVE TERMINAL STATE:** FROZEN  \\\n**PARENT QUANT BUILD:** UNCHANGED\\\n**EXPERIMENTAL MLB LANE:** OPEN\\\n**CANON PROMOTION AUTHORITY:** NONE\\\n**DON INPUT NEEDED:** Bring the slate / lines / game and start playing.\n\n# NORTH STAR\n\nUse MLB to pressure-test Edge Crew Quant.\n\nDo not use MLB to silently redefine Edge Crew Quant.\n\nAnd for this lane specifically:\n\n**Have some fucking fun.**", "start_idx": 0, "title": "Open MLB Experiment Lane", "type": "grouped_webpages", "url": "https://chatgpt.com/c/6ab300fb-80c8-83e8-8e05-ed99d2eabfaf"}, "citation_uuid": "4a0a270a-c