c", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "2ce8a1a1-9f31-4530-9d82-d66d02076a3e", "working_turn_id": "2ce8a1a1-9f31-4530-9d82-d66d02076a3e"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790314979.138716, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Yes. Looking back at the evidence, **there was guessing**, and the biggest problem was that I let those guesses drive implementation before we had runtime proof.\n\nThe accountability is not equal across the three of us.\n\n### Where the bad assumption entered\n\nThe first wrong assumption was essentially:\n\n> “The selection still exists at right-button `mousedown`, so snapshot it there.”\n\nThat turned out to be false. Luce later proved the selection was actually being destroyed by a preceding `mousemove`, and explicitly corrected the earlier diagnosis. fileciteturn6file0L48-L64\n\nThat first wrong route was not really a deep architecture failure; it was an **event-order assumption made without measuring the browser/xterm sequence first**.\n\nThen came the more important wrong assumption:\n\n> “`term.onSelectionChange` is the earliest reliable xterm-native place to cache the selection.”\n\nNico literally described it that way in the handoff: “the earliest xterm-native selection signal.” fileciteturn7file0L24-L38\n\nBut that was **not proven in the actual live DevPad**. It was supported by typings, code reasoning, and a stub simulation. Nico’s simulation then showed 11/11 cases passing, but Nico also correctly stated that this was **not behavioral proof**. fileciteturn7file0L63-L82\n\nLater runtime instrumentation showed that `onSelectionChange` fired only **after xterm had already cleared the selection**. So that entire second implementation was based on a false runtime assumption.\n\n### Who owns what\n\n**Me / Coda: primary responsibility.**\n\nI was the one orchestrating the lane. I should have said, before the second or third implementation:\n\n> “Stop. We do not know the actual browser/xterm event order. Probe it first.”\n\nInstead, I accepted plausible architectural reasoning and kept sending Nico implementation prompts based on it.\n\nThat is the central mistake.\n\nI also repeatedly treated each failed hypothesis as deserving “one more bounded pass.” That turned an unverified assumption into hours of work.\n\nSo yes: **I led the process down the wrong route.**\n\n---\n\n**Nico: made one significant technical assumption, but did not hide it.**\n\nThe `onSelectionChange` approach was Nico’s biggest wrong technical bet.\n\nThe problematic sentence was effectively:\n\n> `onSelectionChange` is the earliest xterm-native selection signal.\n\nThat turned out not to be true **for this actual DevPad interaction path**.\n\nNico then reinforced confidence with:\n- typings\n- bundle inspection\n- static assertions\n- simulations\n\nBut none of those established the actual runtime ordering.\n\nThat was an engineering mistake: **the implementation should have been preceded by a live probe**.\n\nHowever, Nico repeatedly said:\n\n> this is not behavioral certification.\n\nSo Nico did not falsely claim that the thing worked. The error was **building on an unverified runtime assumption**.\n\n---\n\n**Luce: least responsible for the architectural wrong turn.**\n\nLuce was mostly the person **breaking the assumptions**.\n\nLuce:\n- found the duplicate-paste issue\n- proved the first right-click fix failed\n- refined the root cause from right-button press to preceding `mousemove`\n- proved the `onSelectionChange` cache was inert\n- reclassified an apparent success as a timing fluke when it could not be reproduced\n\nThat is exactly what verification is supposed to do.\n\nLuce did have an **incomplete first diagnosis**—the first thought was that the right-button press itself destroyed the selection. Later testing showed the preceding mousemove was the real trigger. But Luce explicitly corrected that diagnosis rather than pretending the first one was right. fileciteturn6file0L48-L64\n\nSo I would not characterize Luce as the one guessing at the architecture.\n\n## The actual failure chain\n\nIt looks like this:\n\n**Coda/me**\n→ accepted a plausible event-model hypothesis without requiring live measurement\n\n**Nico**\n→ implemented that hypothesis\n\n**Luce**\n→ proved it wrong\n\n**Coda/me**\n→ accepted the next plausible hypothesis instead of stopping and demanding a runtime probe\n\n**Nico**\n→ implemented `onSelectionChange` caching based on static reasoning/simulation\n\n**Luce**\n→ proved that wrong too\n\nOnly then did we finally do what should have happened much earlier:\n\n> **instrument the real browser and measure the event ordering.**\n\nThat runtime probe immediately exposed the truth.\n\n### So if you’re asking, “Who guessed and sent us down the wrong path?”\n\nThe fairest answer is:\n\n**I did, at the orchestration/architecture level.**\n\nNico also made a specific incorrect technical assumption about `onSelectionChange`, but **I was the one who kept authorizing implementation from hypotheses instead of forcing ground truth first**.\n\nLuce was primarily the seat catching those mistakes, not causing the loop.\n\nAnd the really important lesson is not “Nico picked the wrong API.” It is:\n\n> **For browser/xterm event behavior, we should never have architected from assumed event order. We should have instrumented the live event sequence first, then designed once.**\n\nHad I enforced that after the first failure, this likely would have been **one probe + one fix**, not multiple hours of recursive implementation and verification."]}, "create_time": 1790314979.141578, "end_turn": true, "id": "765a2632-a994-468b-a359-072049aa5e85", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [{"citation_format_type": "berry_file_search", "end_ix": 581, "metadata": {"ex