running Big Pickle as Coda.**\n\nThose were the actual requested outcomes.\n\nThey were **not**:\n\n- build Rookie DevPad\n- build myDon\n- redesign the DevPad architecture\n- redesign terminal transport\n- investigate xterm internals exhaustively\n- create a new clipboard subsystem\n- build Cable Car/Pigeon\n- perform full resource optimization\n- repeatedly certify every mouse-event permutation\n\nThose entered later as either discussion, future work, or consequences of the debugging process.\n\n---\n\n# B. EXPECTED SIZE OF WORK\n\n### Classification: **BOUNDED MAINTENANCE**\n\nNot an architecture change.\n\nWhy:\n\n**Seat 1** ultimately proved to be almost entirely a configuration change. The existing DevPad already supported an OpenCode seat; Coda's identity home already existed; the tile ID and surrounding architecture did not need replacement.\n\n**Clipboard** was localized primarily to the renderer-side terminal input/clipboard path.\n\nThe eventual investigations showed the relevant path was roughly:\n\n**browser/xterm event → clipboard handler → WebSocket input → `t.write()` → node-pty**\n\nNothing in the original ask inherently required changes to:\n\n- server architecture\n- seat doctrine\n- node-pty\n- transport contracts\n- model architecture\n- inbox architecture\n- framework governance\n\nA sane expectation would have been approximately:\n\n**inspect → patch → load actual board → test real copy/paste → one bounded correction if necessary → close.**\n\n---\n\n# C. ACTUAL WORK PERFORMED\n\nOnly counting things genuinely built, changed, tested, verified, or produced.\n\n## 1. Seat 1 conversion\n\nSeat 1 was changed from the former browser/ChatGPT surface to:\n\n**Coda → OpenCode → Big Pickle**\n\nwith `tileId=1` retained.\n\nThis was genuinely implemented and runtime-observed.\n\n**Classification:** KEEP.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Clipboard path investigated\n\nThe actual terminal clipboard/event path was traced.\n\nThis was useful because it established that clipboard behavior was renderer-side and did not initially require server/PTY architecture changes.\n\n**Classification:** KEEP.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Duplicate paste defect identified\n\nA genuine bug was found:\n\n**custom `term.paste()` behavior and browser-native paste behavior could both execute.**\n\nThis was not imaginary.\n\nNico introduced `preventDefault()` on the relevant paste chords.\n\nLuce later measured the expected single-path behavior in that test environment: one clipboard read, zero native paste events, one PTY frame, one paste result. fileciteturn6file0L28-L45\n\n**Classification:** KEEP.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Right-click selection behavior investigated\n\nA second real defect existed around custom right-click copy.\n\nMultiple implementations were attempted.\n\n### First implementation\n\nSnapshot selection at right-button `mousedown`.\n\nThis worked only while the selection was still alive.\n\nLuce then proved that an intervening `mousemove` could destroy the selection before `mousedown`. fileciteturn6file0L48-L64\n\n### Second implementation\n\nCache the last non-empty selection using:\n\n`term.onSelectionChange(...)`\n\nNico implemented it, passed static validation, and produced code-level simulations.\n\nThe implementation report called this the earliest xterm-native selection signal. fileciteturn7file0L24-L38\n\nThe cache lifecycle was:\n\n- set/refresh on non-empty selection\n- invalidate on non-right interaction\n- consume after copy\n- expire after 5 seconds. fileciteturn7file0L47-L53\n\nStatic simulation reported the expected cases passing. fileciteturn7file0L63-L82\n\nBut live testing later disproved the assumption.\n\n### Third implementation\n\nRuntime instrumentation established that `onSelectionChange` was not producing useful non-empty state during the actual interaction path.\n\nA new implementation used a **capture-phase `mousemove` listener** to observe the selection before xterm's descendant handling destroyed it.\n\nThat