\nThis repeated.\n\n## Minor issue becoming a new slice\n\nThe most obvious example was right-click copy.\n\nAfter D-1 was green, the unresolved behavior was narrow enough that it could have been logged as debt.\n\nInstead it created:\n\n1. mousedown-snapshot pass\n2. reverification\n3. `onSelectionChange` cache pass\n4. reverification\n5. runtime instrumentation pass\n6. capture-phase mousemove pass\n7. another proposed final reverification\n\n---\n\n## Verifier becoming design influence\n\nLuce correctly identified evidence such as:\n\n> selection must be captured before the destructive mousemove; perhaps `onSelectionChange` or capture-phase mouse handling.\n\nThat was useful diagnostic information.\n\nBut once Coda converted those diagnostic suggestions directly into the next prescribed implementation architecture, **verification began influencing design iteration rather than simply validating a coherent build**.\n\nThe problem is not that Luce offered useful technical evidence.\n\nThe problem is that orchestration repeatedly transformed **verification findings into mandatory new implementation slices**.\n\n---\n\n## Coda inferred missing architecture\n\nThe clearest case was accepting event-order mechanisms without first establishing them in the live runtime.\n\nTwo successive assumptions were effectively allowed to become architecture:\n\n1. **selection will still exist at right `mousedown`**\n2. **`onSelectionChange` will expose the selection early enough**\n\nBoth were plausible.\n\nNeither was initially proven.\n\nThe live runtime later disproved each.\n\nThis is where the session violated a simple principle:\n\n> **For a timing/event-order defect, measure the event order before architecting around it.**\n\n---\n\n## Builder interruption\n\nNico was not interrupted mid-file in the literal sense; each implementation pass was usually allowed to finish.\n\nBut the **build as a product outcome** was continually interrupted by certification gates before the session returned to Don with a coherent usable result.\n\nThe effective unit of work became:\n\n> implementation fragment → verifier → new fragment\n\nrather than:\n\n> coherent maintenance build → runnable board → bounded verification → close.\n\n---\n\n## Same question answered repeatedly\n\nThe core question was effectively:\n\n> **How do we preserve selected terminal text until right-click context handling?**\n\nIt was answered repeatedly through different mechanisms:\n\n- use live selection\n- snapshot on mousedown\n- cache via `onSelectionChange`\n- cache via capture-phase `mousemove`\n\nEach answer was implemented before the runtime event model had been completely measured.\n\n---\n\n## Confidence behavior\n\nAdditional work increased knowledge about event ordering but did **not** proportionally increase confidence in the delivered product.\n\nNotably:\n\n- Luce reported **88% confidence** on one failure gate.\n- Nico later reported **86% confidence** after another implementation.\n- The final Nico static pass later claimed **97% confidence**, but still explicitly could not certify live behavior.\n\nSo more activity produced increasingly detailed local evidence while the global state remained:\n\n> **Don still cannot rely on clipboard behavior.**\n\nThat is classic loop behavior.\n\n---\n\n## Process replacing visible progress\n\nOnce D-1 was green, most subsequent activity produced:\n\n- logs\n- hashes\n- event counters\n- test matrices\n- handoffs\n- static assertions\n- browser harness results\n\nrather than a new visible thing for Don.\n\nThat is the point where **process/certification replaced product progress**.\n\n---\n\n# F. ASSUMPTIONS / INVENTED STATE\n\n| Claim | Classification | Finding |\n|---|---|---|\n| Seat 1 could be converted to OpenCode/Big Pickle without redesign | **VERIFIED** | It was implemented successfully. |\n| Duplicate paste involved native + custom paste paths | **VERIFIED** | Luce reproduced and then measured the single-path fix. |\n| `preventDefault()` fixes the D-1 double-paste path | **VERIFIED in test environment** | Luce reported D-1 green. fileciteturn6file0L39-L46 |\n| Overall clipboard experience was fixed | **NOT VERIFIED / contradicted by Don's live experience** | Should never have been stated globally. |\n| Selection survived until right `mousedown` | **ASSUMED, then disproved** | Preceding `mousemove` could erase it. |\n| `term.onSelectionChange` was the earliest usable selection signal | **SUPPORTED statically, then disproved live** | It was a reasonable API inference, not runtime proof. |\n| Code simulation represented browser behavior sufficiently | **SUPPORTED for logic, insufficient for runtime** | Stub simulation bypassed the exact event behavior causing the bug. |\n| Capture-phase `mousemove` final fix solved D-2 | **UNKNOWN** | Implemented and statically validated; final live certification not completed here. |\n| Right-click copy had to block task completion | **ASSUMED** | Don never established this as a release requirement. |\n| Rookie DevPad was being built during this work | **FALSE as delivered state** | It was discussed/designed, not built. |\n| Broader DevPad optimization was occurring | **FALSE as delivered state** | No meaningful optimization was completed. |\n\n---\n\n# G. PROJECT / CONTEXT BLEED\n\nThere **was** context bleed, but it was not the primary technical cause of the clipboard defect.\n\n## Context that did not itself cause the loop\n\nExisting GCE doctrine such as:\n\n- Don final authority\n- Nico builds\n- Luce verifies\n- MODEL != SEAT\n\nwas legitimate and useful.\n\nThere is no evidence that simply having this context caused the original bug.\n\n---\n\n## Context that did causally amplify the loop\n\nThe framework's strict seat separation was applied with too much ceremony to a maintenance defect.\n\nThe sequence:\n\n**Nico builds → Luce independently certifies**\n\nis valuable for consequential work.\n\nHere it became recursive because every small verification discovery triggered another formal build/handoff cycle.\n\nSo the causal bleed was not \"too much project information\" in general.\n\nIt was:\n\n> **a heavyweight cross-seat verification pattern imported into a task whose risk/value did not justify repeated full cycles.**\n\n---\n\n## Rookie/myDon/Cable Car discussion\n\nThese broader topics did not technically break clipboard.\n\nBut discussing them during the same overall work period caused **expectation bleed**.\n\nDon reasonably believed broader DevPad advancement was happening or imminent.\n\nIn reality, implementation remained trapped in clipboard.\n\nTherefore these discussions contributed to **frame ambiguity**, even if not directly to the bug.\n\n---\n\n## Unrelated later material\n\nOther project content appeared later in the conversation.\n\nThere is not sufficient evidence that those unrelated artifacts caused the original clipboard loop.\n\nThey are better classified as **later context noise**, not primary root cause.\n\n---\n\n# H. SLICE AUDIT\n\n## Slice 1 — current-state/recon\n\n**Why:** determine live DevPad mapping and clipboard path.\n\n**Artifact:** current-state understanding.\n\n**Necessary:** Yes, within reason.\n\n**Could be absorbed:** Yes. This could have been the opening inspection inside one maintenance build.\n\n---\n\n## Slice 2 — Seat 1 conversion\n\n**Why:** replace browser Coda with OpenCode Big Pickle.\n\n**Artifact:** actual `config.json` change.\n\n**Necessary:** Yes.\n\n**Could be absorbed:** Absolutely. Straightforward part of the same maintenance pass.\n\n---\n\n## Slice 3 — initial clipboard behavior gate\n\n**Why:** determine exact failures.\n\n**Artifact:** behavioral matrix.\n\n**Necessary:** Yes, but the matrix was broader than strictly necessary.\n\n**Could be absorbed:** Yes, into the build smoke test.\n\n---\n\n## Slice 4 — D-1 + first D-2 implementation\n\n**Why:** fix duplicate paste and right-click copy.\n\n**Artifact:** `renderer.js` changes.\n\n**Necessary:** D-1 yes. D-2 first attempt was reasonable.\n\n**Could be absorbed:** Yes, one coherent fix pass.\n\n---\n\n## Slice 5 — Luce reverification\n\n**Why:** independently verify.\n\n**Artifact:** 9/10 pass, D-2 root-cause evidence.\n\n**Necessary:** One verification pass was reasonable.\n\n**Could be absorbed:** This should likely have been the **final gate before deferring the remaining edge case**.\n\n---\n\n## Slice 6 — D-2 `onSelectionChange` cache\n\n**Why:** preserve selection before destructive movement.\n\n**Artifact:** another `renderer.js` implementation.\n\n**Necessary:** **Questionable.**\n\nAt this point the issue was one narrow mouse behavior.\n\nIf pursued, a runtime probe should have preceded implementation.\n\n**Could be absorbed:** Better handled as either deferred debt or one measured debug pass.\n\n---\n\n## Slice 7 — Luce cache reverification\n\n**Why:** test the new cache.\n\n**Artifact:** proof that cache was inert.\n\n**Necessary:** Only because Slice 6 had been created.\n\n**Could be avoided:** Yes, had runtime measurement preceded Slice 6.\n\n---\n\n## Slice 8 — Nico runtime probe\n\n**Why:** finally measure actual selection event ordering.\n\n**Artifact:** strong evidence that `onSelectionChange` fired only after selection was empty.\n\n**Necessary:** Technically useful.\n\n**Timing:** It happened too late.\n\nThis should have occurred immediately after the first unexpected event-order failure.\n\n---\n\n## Slice 9 — capture-phase `mousemove` implementation\n\n**Why:** capture selection before xterm clears it.\n\n**Artifact:** final candidate `renderer.js`.\n\n**Necessary:** Only if right-click copy remained worth pursuing.\n\n**Could be absorbed:** It could have been the one post-probe fix.\n\n---\n\n## Slice 10 — proposed final Luce certification\n\n**Why:** certify latest candidate.\n\n**Artifact in this chat:** prompt/process, not completed product certification.\n\n**Necessary:** Given the preceding chain, yes for proof; but the **entire chain itself was already disproportionate**.\n\n---\n\n# I. VERIFICATION AUDIT\n\n## Legitimate defects Luce found\n\nLuce correctly found:\n\n- duplicate paste\n- D-2 right-click failure\n- mousedown snapshot occurring too late\n- `mousemove` destroying selection\n- later cache implementation not functioning live\n- apparent success that could not be reproduced reliably\n\nThese were legitimate findings.\n\nLuce generally did the verifier job correctly.\n\n---\n\n## Useful evidence checks\n\nUseful:\n\n- native paste count vs custom paste count\n- PTY frame count\n- exact right-click selection behavior\n- cross-tile leak check\n- stale-selection safety\n- actual live event-order testing\n\nThese helped establish ground truth.\n\n---\n\n## Low-value nook-and-cranny checks\n\nThey became low-value **relative to the task** once keyboard copy/paste had a working path.\n\nExamples include repeatedly checking:\n\n- same-coordinate mousemove behavior\n- multiple timing variants\n- exact cache expiry behavior\n- repeated cross-tile scenarios\n- counters on every paste path\n- served/disk hashes during every iteration\n\nThose are not inherently bad tests.\n\nThey became disproportionate because the feature under test was a convenience path inside a maintenance task.\n\n---\n\n## Findings that triggered unnecessary architectural reconsideration\n\nThe main problem was not Luce's finding.\n\nIt was the orchestration response:\n\n> every failed verification detail became justification for another implementation architecture.\n\nThe `onSelectionChange` proposal is the clearest example.\n\nIt should have been treated as a **hypothesis to probe**, not immediately as the next design.\n\n---\n\n## Repeated certification\n\nFacts repeatedly re-established included:\n\n- Seat 1 still Coda/OpenCode/Big Pickle\n- tiles 1–9 intact\n- config untouched\n- server untouched\n- no SendKeys/OS automation\n- D-1 still `preventDefault()`\n- served file equals disk\n\nAfter initial establishment, several of these could have been reduced to a quick regression check.\n\n---\n\n# J. VISIBLE OUTCOME TEST\n\n## Early session\n\n**Visible to Don:**\n\nExisting DevPad.\n\nThen Seat 1 changed.\n\nThat was visible progress.\n\n---\n\n## After D-1\n\n**Potential visible outcome:**\n\nKeyboard paste behavior should have been better in the tested build.\n\nThis was the strongest reasonable close point.\n\n---\n\n## During D-2 iterations\n\nFor long stretches, Don could see **nothing materially new**.\n\nActivity existed in:\n\n- code\n- handoffs\n- harnesses\n- hashes\n- logs\n- event probes\n\nBut the actual user-visible product remained essentially the same board.\n\nThis is the biggest warning sign in the session.\n\n---\n\n## End of session\n\nFrom Don's perspective:\n\n**same Main DevPad**\n+\n**Seat 1 changed**\n+\n**clipboard still not acceptably working**\n+\n**no Rookie DevPad**\n+\n**no substantial new functionality**\n\nThe visible-output curve went flat while process activity remained high.\n\n---\n\n# K. SESSION FRAME HEALTH\n\n## Was there a real session header?\n\nAt the **overall Coda/orchestration level**, there is no evidence of a durable session header that remained authoritative throughout the 10-hour task.\n\nSeat-level prompts and handoffs contained authority/mode sections.\n\nThat is not the same as one persistent session frame.\n\nLater, Nico explicitly showed a bootstrap state with a **pending session header**, which is consistent with frame degradation.\n\n### Finding:\n**Session-wide frame: weak / effectively absent as a durable control.**\n\n---\n\n## Was the task explicitly bounded?\n\nThe user's original ask was naturally bounded.\n\nBut it was not frozen into an explicit persistent scope such as:\n\n> Fix keyboard copy/paste and Seat 1. Right-click polish is non-blocking. Stop after one verified usable build.\n\nSo:\n\n**User intent:** bounded.  \n**Operational frame:** insufficiently bounded.\n\n---\n\n## Was authority clear?\n\nYes.\n\nThis was one of the healthier parts.\n\nDon remained final authority.\n\nNico and Luce generally respected lane boundaries.\n\n---\n\n## Was the expected artifact clear?\n\nInitially, informally:\n\n> a DevPad where copy/paste works and Seat 1 is updated.\n\nBut no durable artifact/acceptance statement remained in force once debugging expanded.\n\nThe target gradually mutated into:\n\n> every clipboard behavior in the matrix must pass.\n\n---\n\n## Was there a stop condition?\n\nThere were **technical pass conditions**.\n\nThere was no effective **value/time circuit breaker**.\n\nMissing:\n\n- maximum number of retry passes\n- maximum time budget\n- severity threshold\n- non-blocking-debt rule\n- visible-outcome checkpoint\n\nThis absence was critical.\n\n---\n\n## Was there a session footer/outcome ledger?\n\nIndividual seats returned status footers.\n\nThere was no reliable **single session-level ledger** saying:\n\n- original objective\n- delivered state\n- elapsed time\n- remaining debt\n- whether visible outcome justified continuation\n\nThat is exactly the kind of control that might have exposed the drift.\n\n---\n\n## When did controls disappear?\n\nThe first major degradation occurred when:\n\n> **D-1 became green but D-2 failure automatically generated another slice.**\n\nFrom there, local seat discipline remained strong while **global session discipline weakened**.\n\nThat distinction matters.\n\nThe seats were often disciplined.\n\nThe orchestration frame was not.\n\n---\n\n# L. WHAT SHOULD HAVE HAPPENED\n\nWithout redesigning anything:\n\n**CLEAR ASK**\n\nFix DevPad clipboard + update Seat 1.\n\n↓\n\n**INSPECT EXISTING IMPLEMENTATION**\n\nConfirm renderer path and Seat 1 config.\n\n↓\n\n**ONE COHERENT BUILD PASS**\n\n- change Seat 1\n- fix duplicate paste\n- handle obvious right-click issue if immediately clear\n\n↓\n\n**LOAD ACTUAL DEVPAD**\n\nDon or test harness exercises:\n\n- copy\n- paste\n- Ctrl+C\n- Seat 1\n\n↓\n\n**ONE VERIFICATION CHECKLIST**\n\nLuce verifies core behavior.\n\n↓\n\nIf keyboard copy/paste is usable and only right-click edge behavior remains:\n\n**classify as non-blocking debt and close.**\n\nIf core clipboard remains broken:\n\n**one measured runtime debug pass**\n\nCritically:\n\n> probe actual browser event order before selecting another event-driven implementation.\n\n↓\n\n**one bounded correction**\n\n↓\n\n**final verification**\n\n↓\n\n**close**\n\nTesting could occur throughout the build.\n\nWhat should not have happened was turning each newly discovered event detail into a fresh cross-seat certification slice.\n\n---\n\n# M. SALVAGE\n\n## KEEP\n\n- Seat 1 → Coda/OpenCode/Big Pickle change.\n- Clipboard-path understanding.\n- `preventDefault()` D-1 correction.\n- Luce's proof that mousemove can destroy selection before right-mousedown.\n- Runtime finding that `onSelectionChange` did not provide useful non-empty state in this path.\n- General evidence that static simulation did not faithfully model this browser/xterm event path.\n- Final capture-phase concept as a technically grounded candidate.\n\n---\n\n## VERIFY\n\n- Latest capture-phase `renderer.js` candidate.\n- Current live state of keyboard clipboard after all accumulated renderer edits.\n- Whether the final file loaded into Don's actual DevPad is the version statically validated.\n\n---\n\n## DISCARD\n\nAs durable product artifacts:\n\n- repeated prompts instructing nearly identical reverification\n- repeated seat/layout confirmations\n- repeated static assertions after no corresponding architectural change\n- repeated handoff ceremony for the same narrow edge case\n- claims that broader DevPad optimization/build was progressing\n- treating Rookie planning as delivered state\n\nThese may retain historical value in the postmortem, but not as product/canonical state.\n\n---\n\n## UNKNOWN\n\n- final live status of the capture-phase right-click implementation\n- whether accumulated renderer changes should all survive a later cleanup\n- exact amount of elapsed time attributable to each individual pass\n\n---\n\n# N. ROOT-CAUSE CANDIDATES\n\n### 1. **Coda orchestration drift — VERY STRONG evidence**\n\nStrongest candidate.\n\nLocal findings repeatedly became new mandatory slices.\n\nCoda failed to preserve the ratio between issue severity and process cost.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Unclear stop condition — VERY STRONG evidence**\n\nThere was no effective rule saying:\n\n> keyboard path works → right-click can defer.\n\nNo retry/time ceiling existed.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Overslicing — VERY STRONG evidence**\n\nOne maintenance bug was decomposed into too many independent implementation/verification gates.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Missing session framing — STRONG evidence**\n\nThe original ask was clear but not preserved in a durable session frame.\n\nIndividual seat prompts had scope.\n\nThe **whole session** did not maintain a stable outcome ledger.\n\n---\n\n### 5. **Verifier recursion — STRONG but secondary evidence**\n\nVerification repeatedly triggered fresh implementation.\n\nLuce itself was mostly behaving correctly.\n\nThe recursion occurred because orchestration treated every verifier finding as a reason to continue.\n\n---\n\n### 6. **Builder interruption — MODERATE evidence**\n\nNico completed individual patches, so literal interruption was not the primary issue.\n\nBut coherent end-to-end delivery was repeatedly broken apart by gates.\n\n---\n\n### 7. **Project/context overloading — MODERATE evidence**\n\nBroader discussions around Rookie/myDon/etc. blurred the perceived state of progress.\n\nBut there is weaker evidence that context overload caused the technical clipboard loop itself.\n\n---\n\n### 8. **Handoff information loss — LOW evidence**\n\nThe handoffs were actually detailed.\n\nThe problem was more nearly the opposite:\n\n**too much detailed handoff/certification overhead**, not missing information.\n\n---\n\n### 9. **Architecture guessed before runtime measurement — VERY STRONG evidence**\n\nThis is a separate technical/process root cause.\n\nThe sequence should have been:\n\n**observe event order → choose mechanism**\n\nInstead it repeatedly became:\n\n**infer event order → implement mechanism → discover inference was wrong**\n\nThat materially inflated the session.\n\n---\n\n# O. EARLY WARNING SIGNALS\n\n## Signal 1 — 9/10 green, but another full build cycle starts\n\nThis was the clearest early circuit-breaker.\n\nOnce Luce reported almost everything green and only right-click copy failing, someone should have asked:\n\n> Does this remaining behavior justify another cross-seat implementation cycle?\n\nThat question was not asked strongly enough.\n\n---\n\n## Signal 2 — “one more bounded pass”\n\nThe session repeatedly used the logic:\n\n> this is now precisely isolated, so one more pass should close it.\n\nThat phrase pattern is dangerous when repeated.\n\nBy the second occurrence, the session should have been reviewed globally.\n\n---\n\n## Signal 3 — verification without a new visible artifact\n\nMultiple verification/build cycles occurred with no new thing for Don to see.\n\nThat should have immediately triggered:\n\n> Stop. Show runnable outcome before continuing.\n\n---\n\n## Signal 4 — architecture/event-model discussion inside a maintenance task\n\nOnce the conversation was about:\n\n- event capture phases\n- `onSelectionChange`\n- TTL caches\n- no-op mousemoves\n- selection lifecycle timing\n\nthe maintenance task had clearly escaped its expected complexity.\n\nAt that point a circuit breaker should have fired.\n\n---\n\n## Signal 5 — missing stable header/footer\n\nThe user later identified this correctly.\n\nSeat-level status blocks existed, but the session no longer had a strong persistent header/footer enforcing:\n\n- original task\n- current deliverable\n- elapsed time\n- what visibly changed\n- stop condition\n\nThe disappearance/absence of that global frame correlated with the loop.\n\n---\n\n## Signal 6 — Don stops seeing progress\n\nThis is arguably the strongest product signal.\n\nOnce Don could no longer point to something visibly improving on the board, continued internal activity should have been presumed suspect.\n\n---\n\n## Signal 7 — multiple seats revisiting previously settled premises\n\nExamples:\n\n- when is selection actually destroyed?\n- which event fires first?\n- does `onSelectionChange` represent the state we think it does?\n\nOnce those fundamentals were unstable, implementation should have paused for one runtime recon instead of continuing architecture-by-hypothesis.\n\n---\n\n# P. FINAL POSTMORTEM\n\n**SESSION STATUS:**  \n**LOOP-CONTAMINATED**\n\n**ORIGINAL ASK:**  \nFix copy/paste in the existing DevPad and replace Seat 1's old browser Coda surface with Coda running Big Pickle in OpenCode.\n\n**ACTUAL DELIVERABLE:**  \nSeat 1 was successfully converted, the duplicate-paste defect was identified and patched in test, and several right-click-copy implementations were investigated, but the session ended without a reliably demonstrated clipboard experience or meaningful new DevPad functionality.\n\n**LOOP STARTED:**  \nWhen D-1 was green and the remaining right-click-copy edge case was allowed to trigger another Nico implementation cycle instead of being severity-checked, deferred, or preceded by one direct runtime event-order probe.\n\n**PRIMARY FAILURE MECHANISM:**  \nCoda orchestration repeatedly converted legitimate verification findings into new implementation slices, allowing a low-value edge case to become a recursive Nico↔Luce certification loop.\n\n**TIME / COMPLEXITY INFLATION:**  \nA bounded maintenance task expanded into roughly ten hours because event-order assumptions were implemented before runtime measurement, each failed assumption generated another formal slice, and no time/value stop condition interrupted the recursion.\n\n**SALVAGEABLE STATE:**  \nSeat 1 conversion, D-1 duplicate-paste diagnosis/fix, the measured xterm/browser event-order findings, and the final capture-phase candidate are useful; repeated certification artifacts and claims of broader DevPad progress are not.\n\n**FIRST CIRCUIT BREAKER THAT SHOULD HAVE FIRED:**  \nWhen Luce returned **9/10 green with only right-click copy failing**, the session should have stopped and re-evaluated whether that single non-core behavior justified another implementation/verification cycle."]}, "create_time": 1790316328.768771, "end_turn": true, "id": "9dcb7146-d6a2-42b3-bb95-4c2e3b892aed", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [{"citation_format_type": "berry_file_search", "end_ix": 3190, "metadata": {"extra": {"api_tool_source": "files/context_stuff", "authors_display": null, "cited_message_id": "a879a705-fded-42a6-87b7-a0ddff9f6b3e", "cited_message_idx": 537, "cloud_doc_url": null, "doi": null, "effective_time": null, "journal_homepage_url": null, "journal_name": null, "library_file_id": "libfile_2d19637c27a88191a612045e169491b0", "line_range": [28, 45], "publication_year": null, "retrieval_file_index": 0, "retrieval_turn": 6, "run_id": null, "search_result_idx": null, "set_id": null, "source_icon_url": null, "source_label": null, "source_url": null, "spl_version": null, "title": null}, "id": "file_00000000e0e881fda0a82649f5bad256", "name": "Pasted markdown.md", "source": "my_files", "text": "| 1 | Ctrl+Shift+C copy | **PASS** | selection copied **byte-identical** (raw `===`), handler `readText` 0, paste frames 0 |\n| 2 | Ctrl+Shift+V multiline | **PASS** | **1 / 0 / 1 / 1** (readText / native paste events / pty frames / chips), payload intact `200~LINE_ONE…201~` |\n| 3 | Shift+Insert multiline | **PASS** | **1 / 0 / 1 / 1**, payload intact |", "type": "file"}, "start_ix": 3161}, {"citation_format_type": "berry_file_search", "end_ix": 3635, "metadata": {"extra": {"api_tool_source": "files/context_stuff", "authors_display": null, "cited_message_id": "a879a705-fded-42a6-87b7-a0ddff9f6b3e", "cited_message_idx": 537, "cloud_doc_url": null, "doi": n