er_deny": [], "parent_id": "4893de73-409c-4f1d-a5af-3d7859dc8bb2", "reasoning_end_time": 1790218027.0537438, "reasoning_recap_type": "hide_all", "reasoning_start_time": 1790218021.889223, "reasoning_status": "reasoning_ended", "request_id": "7e37ee57-731b-40db-a39d-fd16cfcd7be2", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "b66a7b41-db6d-4872-a1bf-5187a26bd18f", "working_turn_id": "b66a7b41-db6d-4872-a1bf-5187a26bd18f"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": null, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {"real_author": "tool:web"}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Exploratory only: **Xiaomi 15 vs OnePlus 13 is actually a very clean comparison**, because both are Snapdragon 8 Elite flagships but they optimize for different things. citeturn732579search0turn732579search1\n\n| | **Xiaomi 15** | **OnePlus 13** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Character | Compact flagship | Big performance flagship |\n| Display | 6.36\", 1–120 Hz AMOLED | ~6.8\", 2K 120 Hz ProXDR |\n| Processor | Snapdragon 8 Elite | Snapdragon 8 Elite |\n| Battery | 5240 mAh | Larger silicon-carbon class battery |\n| Wired charging | 90W | 80W US model |\n| Wireless | 50W | 50W |\n| Camera identity | **Leica**, 3×50 MP system | **Hasselblad**, 3×50 MP system |\n| OS | HyperOS | OxygenOS |\n| U.S. ownership | Import/global model | **Official U.S. product/support** |\n| Physical feel | Smaller, 191 g, 71.2 mm wide | Bigger, more “power-user slab” |\n| Current rough street price | ~$600–700 global import | ~$700–850 unlocked |\n\nXiaomi is unusually attractive if you want a **smaller flagship**. The 6.36-inch screen, 191 g body, Leica camera system, 5240 mAh battery, 90W wired/50W wireless charging and Snapdragon 8 Elite are a ridiculous amount of hardware in that footprint. citeturn732579search1turn732579search2\n\nproduct[\"turn732579product0\",\"Xiaomi 15 5G Global 12/512GB\",{\"render_as\":\"block\"}]\n\nThe **OnePlus 13**, though, is probably the easier American daily-driver experiment. OnePlus sells and supports it directly in the U.S.; it has Snapdragon 8 Elite, IP68/IP69 resistance, 80W wired and 50W wireless charging, a 2K high-refresh display, and the Hasselblad triple-camera setup. You avoid most of the imported-phone questions around carrier provisioning, warranty and repair. citeturn732579search0turn732579search3\n\nproduct[\"turn732579product5\",\"OnePlus 13 512GB Unlocked\",{\"render_as\":\"block\"}]\n\nAnd there is a third angle I think you'd enjoy exploring: **OnePlus 13R**. That's the “how much phone can we get before flagship pricing becomes stupid?” route. Current unlocked listings are around **$490**, dramatically below either flagship. entity[\"turn732579product1\",\"OnePlus 13R 256GB Unlocked\"]\n\nSo conceptually:\n\n**Xiaomi 15 = coolest hardware experiment.** Compact, Leica, HyperOS, Xiaomi/MiMo connection, less common in the U.S.\n\n**OnePlus 13 = safest enthusiast Android.** Big, extremely fast, OxygenOS, proper U.S. support, fewer carrier headaches.\n\n**OnePlus 13R = value play.** I'd investigate this if the objective is “fun Android/GCE terminal device without dropping $800.”\n\nThe interesting part for **your DevPad/mobile-command-center idea** is that Xiaomi's smaller body might actually be nicer as a phone, while the OnePlus 13's huge display is probably better if you're living in terminals, dashboards, remote desktop, SSH and ChatGPT all day.\n\nIf we're just kicking tires, **I'd explore OnePlus 13R → Xiaomi 15 → OnePlus 13**, because those three tell us whether you value price, compactness, or giant-screen power most."]}, "create_time": 1790218025.874012, "end_turn": true, "id": "e057b8df-e3f4-4ac1-9a7b-4369e34123bc", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [{"alt": "([OnePlus](https://www.oneplus.com/us/13?utm_source=chatgpt.com))", "end_idx": 211