{"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Yep — I see the failure mode.\n\n**The seat/identity layer is correct. The launch/runtime layer regressed.** The screenshot shows the identity homes are right (`GCE_IDENTITY\\Chiave`, `Nico`, `Luce`, etc.), but every OpenCode pane is falling through to the same **Big Pickle default**. And Vittorio got converted from the working **Kimi subscription launch path** into OpenCode, which is exactly what we did *not* want.\n\nMy working hypothesis is that the `model` value in the DevPad JSON is being treated as **display/config metadata**, but the OpenCode launcher is not actually translating it into whatever OpenCode requires at process launch/session creation. Kimi worked because its launch spec was explicit: launch the Kimi CLI in the correct identity home. So we should tell Nico to **restore the known-good Kimi pattern first**, then fix OpenCode model selection by observing how OpenCode itself accepts the model—not by guessing.\n\nGive Big Pickle/Nico this:\n\n```text\nGCE DEVPAD — RUNTIME / MODEL BINDING CORRECTIVE PASS\n\nAuthority: Don\nSeat: Nico\nMode: DIAGNOSE + BOUNDED CORRECTION\nTarget: C:\\Users\\Dekan AI Brother\\GCE_DON\\command-center\\\n\nCURRENT OBSERVED STATE\n\nThe latest DevPad relaunch proves an important split:\n\nGOOD:\n- Seat placement is substantially correct.\n- Identity homes are correct.\n- OpenCode panes are launching under the correct GCE_IDENTITY\\<Seat> directories.\n- VoiceBridge is healthy.\n- The 3×3 board is rendering.\n\nBAD:\n- Runtime/model binding regressed.\n- Nearly every agent pane is launching OpenCode with the same Big Pickle default model.\n- Vittorio lost the previously working Kimi subscription/CLI launch and is now incorrectly launching OpenCode.\n- The model values configured in DevPad appear not to be controlling the actual OpenCode runtime/model.\n\nDO NOT redesign DevPad.\n\nThis is a launch-spec/runtime-binding bug.\n\n==================================================\n1. RESTORE VITTORIO FIRST\n==================================================\n\nVittorio must NOT be OpenCode in this configuration.\n\nBinding from Don:\n\nVittorio\n→ Kimi subscription / Kimi CLI\n→ Vittorio identity/bootstrap\n→ C:\\Users\\Dekan AI Brother\\GCE_IDENTITY\\Vittorio\n\nWe had this working previously.\n\nFind the prior/known-good Kimi launch mechanism already present in the Command Center code/config/history and restore that exact pattern.\n\nDo not emulate Kimi through OpenCode if the native Kimi subscription CLI path already works.\n\nVerify:\n- executable/runtime is Kimi\n- CWD/identity home is Vittorio\n- Vittorio bootstrap/identity loads\n- Kimi subscription session works\n\nMODEL != SEAT remains binding.\n\n==================================================\n2. DIAGNOSE OPENCode MODEL SELECTION\n==================================================\n\nCurrent symptom:\n\nCorrect seat identity/CWD\n+\nOpenCode launches\n+\nall panes inherit Big Pickle\n\nTherefore do NOT assume config.json's model field is actually applied.\n\nTrace the full launch chain:\n\nconfig.json\n→ seats.js\n→ adapter.js\n→ adapters/opencode.js\n→ resolved launch spec\n→ spawned OpenCode command/process\n\nDetermine exactly:\n\nA. What model/provider values exist in config.\nB. Whether those fields survive resolveLaunchSpec.\nC. Whether they are passed to OpenCode at launch.\nD. Whether OpenCode supports model selection by:\n   - command-line flag\n   - environment variable\n   - per-project config\n   - session initialization\n   - another existing supported mechanism.\n\nDO NOT GUESS THE FLAG OR SYNTAX.\n\nInspect:\n- current OpenCode adapter\n- existing working launch commands\n- `opencode --help` / local CLI help\n- current OpenCode config\n- any prior DevPad implementation that successfully selected a non-default model.\n\nWe need observed local behavior, not documentation assumptions.\n\n==================================================\n3. IMPORTANT CONFIG RULE\n==================================================\n\nIf DevPad currently contains fields like:\n\nseat\nprovider\nmodel\ncwd\n\nbut the launcher only consumes seat/cwd, then report that explicitly.\n\nA JSON field that says:\n\nmodel: \"glm-5.1\"\n\ndoes NOT count as working unless the spawned OpenCode session actually starts on GLM 5.1.\n\nThe UI must reflect runtime truth, not desired config.\n\n==================================================\n4. REQUIRED SEAT/RUNTIME TARGETS\n==================================================\n\nPreserve the current corrected board.\n\nROW 1\n1 Coda\n2 Chiave\n3 Vittorio\n\nROW 2\n4 Nico\n5 VoiceBridge\n6 Luce\n\nROW 3\n7 Talon\n8 Keel\n9 Falcon\n\nBindings explicitly specified by Don:\n\nCoda\n→ Browser / ChatGPT\n→ persistent authenticated browser profile\n\nChiave\n→ OpenCode\n→ Chiave identity/bootstrap\n→ GLM 5.1\n\nVittorio\n→ Kimi native/subscription CLI\n→ Vittorio identity/bootstrap\n\nNico\n→ OpenCode\n→ Nico identity/bootstrap\n→ retain currently approved model unless another binding is already explicitly configured\n\nVoiceBridge\n→ existing VoiceBridge service\n\nLuce\n→ OpenCode\n→ Luce identity/bootstrap\n→ Big Pickle\n\nTalon\n→ OpenCode\n→ Talon identity/bootstrap\n→ use its configured/approved binding, but VERIFY actual runtime\n\nKeel\n→ OpenCode\n→ Keel identity/bootstrap\n→ use its configured/approved binding, but VERIFY actual runtime\n\nFalcon\n→ OpenCode\n→ Falcon identity/bootstrap\n→ use its configured/approved binding, but VERIFY actual runtime\n\nDo not assign new models to Nico/Talon/Keel/Falcon merely because Big Pickle is currently appearing.\n\nIf their desired model is not explicitly established in current authority/config, preserve UNKNOWN / CURRENT until Don decides.\n\n==================================================\n5. FIX OPENCode LAUNCH BINDING\n==================================================\n\nOnce you identify the actual supported local OpenCode model-selection mechanism:\n\nImplement the smallest change so a DevPad seat launch can explicitly request its configured provider/model.\n\nDesired conceptual contract:\n\nseat config:\n  seat identity\n  cwd\n  runtime = opencode\n  provider\n  model\n\nlaunch adapter:\n  validates runtime/provider/model\n  converts them into the ACTUAL OpenCode-supported invocation/config mechanism\n\nspawned process:\n  opens under seat identity CWD\n  uses requested model/provider\n\nDo not hardcode Chiave or Luce logic into server.js if the existing adapter/config system can express it generically.\n\nPrefer fixing the OpenCode adapter once.\n\n==================================================\n6. PROVE IT WITH TWO DIFFERENT MODELS\n==================================================\n\nThe regression is not fixed merely because OpenCode launches.\n\nProve model binding by launching at least TWO OpenCode seats with intentionally different bindings.\n\nMinimum proof:\n\nChiave\n→ OpenCode\n→ GLM 5.1\n\nLuce\n→ OpenCode\n→ Big Pickle\n\nThen inspect the actual running/session state and prove they are different models.\n\nThe pane label/config alone is insufficient evidence.\n\nIf OpenCode exposes session/model state, use it.\n\n==================================================\n7. PRESERVE WORKING IDENTITY BEHAVIOR\n==================================================\n\nDo not disturb the part that is working.\n\nCurrent identity-home behavior is good:\n\nGCE_IDENTITY\\Chiave\nGCE_IDENTITY\\Nico\nGCE_IDENTITY\\Luce\nGCE_IDENTITY\\Talon\nGCE_IDENTITY\\Keel\nGCE_IDENTITY\\Falcon\netc.\n\nThe correction must preserve:\n\ncorrect seat\n→ correct identity home\n→ correct bootstrap\n→ correct runtime\n→ correct model/provider\n\nThis is a four-layer binding.\n\nDo not solve model selection by flattening identity.\n\n==================================================\n8. KIMI IS THE REFERENCE IMPLEMENTATION\n==================================================\n\nThe previous Kimi setup is useful evidence.\n\nIt successfully demonstrated:\n\nseat\n→ correct identity home\n→ explicit runtime executable\n→ correct subscription/runtime behavior\n\nUse that launch architecture as a reference for how explicit runtime binding should work.\n\nDo not replace working native Kimi with OpenCode merely to make everything uniform.\n\nUniformity is NOT the goal.\n\nCorrect runtime per seat is the goal.\n\n==================================================\n9. DO NOT TOUCH\n==================================================\n\nDo not:\n- rebuild inventory\n- redesign the grid\n- change VoiceBridge\n- change numeric tile addressing\n- modify ECQ\n- alter seat doctrine\n- rewrite identities\n- convert all runtimes to OpenCode\n- rotate credentials\n- inspect secret values\n- invent model substitutions\n\n==================================================\n10. STOP CONDITIONS\n==================================================\n\nStop and return to Don if:\n\n- OpenCode cannot accept an explicit model at launch/session creation\n- GLM 5.1 is unavailable through the currently configured OpenCode providers\n- restoring native Kimi requires a login/subscription action from Don\n- a provider credential is missing\n- the desired model for Nico/Talon/Keel/Falcon is not explicitly established\n- correcting model selection requires broad architecture work\n\nDo not silently fall back to Big Pickle.\n\nA failed requested model must be visible as FAILED/BLOCKED, not replaced invisibly by the default model.\n\n==================================================\n11. REQUIRED RETURN\n==================================================\n\nRESULT:\nPASS / PARTIAL / BLOCKED\n\nROOT CAUSE:\nExplain exactly why all OpenCode panes defaulted to Big Pickle.\n\nVITTORIO:\nConfirm native Kimi restored or state blocker.\n\nOPENCode MODEL BINDING:\nExplain the actual supported local mechanism discovered.\n\nFILES CHANGED:\nExact files and purpose.\n\nPROOF:\nReturn actual observed runtime/model for:\n\nChiave\nLuce\nVittorio\n\nThen return current runtime/model for:\nNico\nTalon\nKeel\nFalcon\n\nDo not report config intent as runtime proof.\n\nFINAL VERDICT:\nOne of:\n\nDEVPAD RUNTIME BINDINGS CORRECT AND VERIFIED\n\nDEVPAD IDENTITIES CORRECT — MODEL BINDING PARTIAL\n\nKIMI RESTORED — OPENCode MODEL BINDING BLOCKED\n\nBLOCKED\n\nDo not proceed into additional DevPad features after this pass.\n```\n\nAnd I’d tell Nico one more thing outside the prompt: **don’t “fix” this by putting GLM or Kimi names in the tile headers.** We need the actual spawned runtime to prove it.\n\nThe good news is that this is narrower than it looks. **The hard part—seat → identity home—is working.** We need to repair the final hop:\n\n**identity → launcher → provider/model.**"]}, "create_time": 1790227767.858995, "end_turn": true, "id": "006133e2-0f40-4584-b5d3-ba3b332d0378", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "code_blocks": {"0": {"edited": false, "id": "22c22j", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}}, "content_references": [], "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finish_details": {"stop_tokens": [200002], "type": "stop"}, "gizmo_id": "g-p-6ab41b7c2620819187c626358eeb8a48", "is_complete": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "f7597c61-02d7-4075-9fe1-8ef900f2af5e", "request_id": "49d7a64b-8d2f-4e41-94b8-256eb54de836", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_result_groups": [], "story_events": [], "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "7d5fff34-c0af-409a-8168-58df891da676", "working_turn_id": "7d5fff34-c0af-409a-8168-58df891da676"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790227824.622844, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "user"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["I think the cleanest way is to build another dev pad. For our little myDawn control orchestration. And having it like we had before, like the inbox queue. Where it shows Don the handoffs and who it routes to. With the routing, and I just click and it sends, and I click and it sends, and I click and it sends. So this way I can click through. And it should route to the right person and then ping the terminal in DevPath. Based off of identity and routing. Through the MCP we did for the inbox. How hard of a lift is that? That shouldn't be that hard. "]}, "create_time": 1790228271.47, "end_turn": null, "id": "917214b1-364c-4969-90e3-078031cd298d", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "message_source": null, "request_id": "1191b778-26a0-4ec5-85ba-82e933a5da88", "selected_sources": [], "serialization_metadata": {"custom_symbol_offsets": []}, "turn_exchange_id": "eb31e75d-eabe-45be-8942-88e82d3a6d44", "working_turn_id": "eb31e75d-eabe-45be-8942-88e82d3a6d44"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790228293.176149, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["No, that should **not** be a hard lift if we keep the scope disciplined.\n\nWhat you’re describing is basically a **Don orchestration pad** sitting on top of infrastructure we already have:\n\n**Don Control Pad → inbox queue/MCP → route by seat identity → DevPad target → ping/focus target terminal**\n\nThe key reason it’s tractable is that we are **not inventing routing from scratch**. We already have:\n\n- seat identities\n- DevPad tile identity\n- inbox MCP plumbing\n- queue concepts\n- numeric tile addressing\n- process/session inventory\n- a main Command Center that knows which pane belongs to which seat\n\nSo this becomes mostly a **thin control surface + routing adapter**.\n\n## What I would build first\n\nKeep v1 brutally simple:\n\n- left side: **handoff queue**\n- each item shows:\n  - subject\n  - source\n  - target seat\n  - priority/status\n- one-click buttons:\n  - **Route**\n  - **Route + Focus**\n  - maybe **Hold**\n- route action:\n  1. write structured handoff into target seat inbox\n  2. mark queue item sent\n  3. call DevPad API with target seat/tile ID\n  4. focus/ping the appropriate terminal\n\nThat’s enough to give you the workflow you want:\n\n**click → send → next → click → send → next**\n\n## The clean architecture\n\nI’d keep it:\n\n**myDon Control**\n→ local HTTP/API\n→ inbox MCP / queue writer\n→ seat-routing map\n→ main DevPad control endpoint\n→ target tile focus/ping\n\nThe important thing is **seat is the routing key**.\n\nNot:\n- PID\n- current model\n- current provider\n- window order\n\nBecause those can change.\n\nSo:\n\n**Luce handoff → seat=luce → inbox path/API → tile 6 → focus Luce pane**\n\nThat is stable.\n\n## Hardest part\n\nThe only slightly annoying part is **“ping the terminal”** semantics.\n\nWriting to the inbox is easy.\n\nFocusing the right tile is also pretty straightforward if the main DevPad already knows tile IDs.\n\nActually notifying the running agent immediately depends on how we want the terminal to react:\n\n- simple focus only\n- inject a lightweight “inbox updated” control message\n- or have each agent poll/watch its inbox\n\nI would start with **focus + visible inbox badge**.\n\nThen add actual wake/ping behavior once we know what each runtime supports safely.\n\n## Effort estimate\n\nIf Nico/Big Pickle stays disciplined:\n\n- **v1 queue + route + focus:** small-to-medium lift\n- **seat-aware inbox routing:** small\n- **UI:** small\n- **reliable focus/ping across mixed runtimes:** medium\n- **full autonomous inter-agent routing:** later, and not needed now\n\nSo yes — **this is very buildable without turning into a science project**.\n\nThe smart move is to build it as a separate small app:\n\n**`myDon` / `Don Controller`**\n\nnot cram it into the main DevPad.\n\nThen the main DevPad stays the **work surface**, and myDon becomes the **routing/control surface**."]}, "create_time": 1790228274.672972, "end_turn": true, "id": "3de916e8-228f-4031-b535-c3a84d690fd6", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [], "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finish_details": {"stop_tokens": [200002], "type": "stop"}, "gizmo_id": "g-p-6ab41b7c2620819187c626358eeb8a48", "is_complete": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "ccfe2804-d8a9-4065-bbc5-d8dba51de3cd", "request_id": "1191b778-26a0-4ec5-85ba-82e933a5da88", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_result_groups": [], "story_events": [], "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "eb31e75d-eabe-45be-8942-88e82d3a6d44", "working_turn_id": "eb31e75d-eabe-45be-8942-88e82d3a6d44"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790228292.155772, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "user"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Yeah, so do Don a favor. What I'll do, give Don a super duper nice detailed spec and handoff and I'll give it to GLM chat real quick. But you got to be detailed because this— he doesn't have the framework, he doesn't have everything because it's GLM chat. And we'll have them write up a quick architecture spec. "]}, "create_time": 1790228320.573, "end_turn": null, "id": "51e00b42-eb32-4ba8-92d3-b455e0c86ada", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "message_source": null, "request_id": "413975a6-2af6-4531-a6f5-884fdb2f416d", "selected_sources": [], "serialization_metadata": {"custom_symbol_offsets": []}, "turn_exchange_id": "422e7a4a-fd25-4775-b60b-a0bf8ca12db6", "working_turn_id": "422e7a4a-fd25-4775-b60b-a0bf8ca12db6"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790228402.45585, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content": "Worked for a couple of seconds", "content_type": "reasoning_recap"}, "create_time": 1790228328.13255, "end_turn": false, "id": "fff9049f-67af-422d-a8cd-235bbb71018d", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": false, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finished_duration_sec": 1, "hide_inline_actions": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "a409647d-e9c2-46ba-b6d6-481a54e0dc0c", "reasoning_end_time": 1790228328.1115701, "reasoning_recap_type": "hide_all", "reasoning_start_time": 1790228326.3332613, "reasoning_status": "reasoning_ended", "request_id": "413975a6-2af6-4531-a6f5-884fdb2f416d", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "422e7a4a-fd25-4775-b60b-a0bf8ca12db6", "working_turn_id": "422e7a4a-fd25-4775-b60b-a0bf8ca12db6"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": null, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Absolutely. Here’s a **self-contained architecture handoff/spec prompt** you can drop straight into GLM Chat. I’ve written it so GLM does **not** need prior framework context to understand the system, the operating doctrine, the current implementation, or the exact architecture question.\n\n```text\nGCE / myDon CONTROL ORCHESTRATION PAD\nARCHITECTURE SPEC REQUEST FOR GLM\n\nAuthority: Don\nPurpose: Produce a concise but technically serious architecture specification for a lightweight personal orchestration/control surface that sits above an existing DevPad Command Center.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nYou are not designing the entire GCE framework.\nYou are being given enough context to architect one bounded subsystem cleanly.\n\nThe requested subsystem is called:\n\nmyDon Control\nor\nDon Controller\n\nThe name is provisional. Focus on architecture, not branding.\n\n==================================================\n1. HIGH-LEVEL SYSTEM CONTEXT\n==================================================\n\nDon operates a local multi-agent / multi-runtime environment on Windows.\n\nThere is already a working local application called the GCE DevPad Command Center.\n\nCanonical path:\n\nC:\\Users\\Dekan AI Brother\\GCE_DON\\command-center\\\n\nThe DevPad Command Center is a local browser-based control/work surface.\n\nCurrent implementation characteristics:\n\n- Node.js backend\n- HTTP server\n- WebSocket support\n- xterm.js terminal panes\n- node-pty terminal spawning\n- seat registry/config\n- runtime adapters\n- process/session inventory\n- VoiceBridge integration\n- inbox MCP plumbing\n- seat-specific identity homes\n- browser/terminal/service panes\n- local port currently centered around :4727\n\nDevPad is intended to be the WORK SURFACE.\n\nThe new subsystem, myDon Control, should be the ORCHESTRATION SURFACE.\n\nDo not merge those concepts unnecessarily.\n\n==================================================\n2. CORE OPERATING DOCTRINE\n==================================================\n\nThe GCE system intentionally separates:\n\nIDENTITY\nROLE\nSEAT\nMODEL\nRUNTIME\nPROVIDER\nPROJECT\nAUTHORITY\n\nThese are NOT interchangeable.\n\nBinding principle:\n\nMODEL != SEAT\n\nExamples:\n\n- A seat named Luce may currently run Big Pickle.\n- Vittorio may currently run Kimi.\n- Chiave may currently run GLM.\n- A future model swap must not change the seat identity.\n- A PID must never define seat identity.\n- A provider must never define seat identity.\n- A runtime process must never define authority.\n\nThe SEAT is the stable routing identity.\n\nThis is important because myDon Control must route work by seat identity, not by current process/model/provider.\n\n==================================================\n3. CURRENT MAIN DEVPAD BOARD\n==================================================\n\nCurrent intended main 3x3 operational board:\n\nROW 1\n1 = Coda\n2 = Chiave\n3 = Vittorio\n\nROW 2\n4 = Nico\n5 = VoiceBridge\n6 = Luce\n\nROW 3\n7 = Talon\n8 = Keel\n9 = Falcon\n\nCurrent rough semantics:\n\nCoda\n- strategic advisor / governance / canon\n- browser / ChatGPT surface\n\nChiave\n- architecture seat\n- intended OpenCode lane\n- current target model may be GLM 5.1\n\nVittorio\n- technical advisor / warboard / challenge\n- currently a strong candidate for native Kimi subscription/runtime\n\nNico\n- implementation builder / implementation hammer\n- typically code/build work\n\nVoiceBridge\n- Don’s voice control / dictation / command bridge\n- service lane, not a conventional AI seat\n\nLuce\n- verification / ground truth\n- independent truth/verification lane\n\nTalon\n- operations / routing / coordination\n\nKeel\n- build / deployment / test / evidence / stability support\n\nFalcon\n- data acquisition / retrieval / operational data support\n\nThe exact runtime/model attached to a seat may change over time.\n\nThe seat identity should remain stable.\n\n==================================================\n4. NUMERIC TILE IDENTITY\n==================================================\n\nDevPad is also moving toward stable numeric addressing.\n\nCurrent intended mapping:\n\n1 Coda\n2 Chiave\n3 Vittorio\n4 Nico\n5 VoiceBridge\n6 Luce\n7 Talon\n8 Keel\n9 Falcon\n\nThis numeric ID is a navigation/control alias.\n\nIt does NOT replace the seat identity.\n\nConceptually:\n\ntile number != seat != model != runtime\n\nVoiceBridge may eventually support commands like:\n\n\"number 6\"\n\"focus 6\"\n\"open 6\"\n\"send this to 7\"\n\nThe numeric map should be stable enough for muscle memory, but routing authority should still resolve through the seat registry.\n\n==================================================\n5. EXISTING INBOX / HANDOFF CONCEPT\n==================================================\n\nThere is already an inbox / command-queue concept in the Command Center.\n\nExisting implementation areas include:\n\n- inbox MCP\n- ai-inbox\n- queue concepts\n- handoff-style structured messages\n- per-seat routing concepts\n\nThe exact historical implementation may be imperfect, but the important architectural fact is:\n\nWe already have a local MCP/inbox mechanism that can be used as the durable delivery path.\n\nThe new myDon Control application should reuse this rather than inventing a second incompatible messaging system.\n\nThink:\n\nmyDon Control\n    -> handoff object\n    -> inbox/queue API or MCP adapter\n    -> seat-specific target inbox\n    -> DevPad target notification/focus\n\n==================================================\n6. WHAT DON WANTS TO DO\n==================================================\n\nDon wants a lightweight controller where he can quickly review pending handoffs and route them.\n\nThe desired interaction is intentionally simple:\n\nCLICK\n-> SEND\n-> NEXT\n-> CLICK\n-> SEND\n-> NEXT\n\nThe controller should visually show:\n\n- what the handoff is\n- where it came from\n- who it is going to\n- status\n- maybe priority\n- maybe project/workstream\n- maybe a short context summary\n\nDon should be able to route a handoff to a seat with one click.\n\nExample:\n\nIncoming handoff:\n\"Verify DevPad model binding\"\n\nSuggested target:\nLuce\n\nDon clicks:\nROUTE TO LUCE\n\nSystem should then:\n\n1. create/send a structured handoff into Luce’s inbox\n2. record that the handoff was routed\n3. optionally focus or ping Luce’s tile in DevPad\n4. preserve an audit trail\n5. show that the item is now SENT / ROUTED\n\nThe first version does NOT need autonomous agent-to-agent orchestration.\n\nDon remains in the loop.\n\n==================================================\n7. myDon CONTROL — PURPOSE\n==================================================\n\nmyDon Control should be a thin orchestration surface.\n\nIt is NOT another full terminal wall.\n\nIt should NOT duplicate the main DevPad.\n\nMain DevPad:\n- terminals\n- seat identity\n- runtimes\n- logs\n- active work\n- inventory\n- status\n\nmyDon Control:\n- handoff queue\n- routing\n- seat selection\n- focus/open\n- inbox status\n- quick actions\n- voice-friendly command targeting\n- optional current-context send\n\nThink of it as a compact cockpit remote for Don.\n\n==================================================\n8. DESIRED V1 FEATURE SET\n==================================================\n\nV1 should be deliberately small.\n\nRequired:\n\nA. HANDOFF QUEUE VIEW\nShow queued handoffs in a simple ordered list.\n\nMinimum fields:\n\n- handoff ID\n- subject/title\n- source\n- target seat\n- project/workstream\n- priority\n- status\n- created timestamp\n- short context/summary\n- maybe evidence/source link/path if relevant\n\nB. ROUTE ACTION\nOne click:\n\nRoute to:\n- Coda\n- Chiave\n- Vittorio\n- Nico\n- Luce\n- Talon\n- Keel\n- Falcon\n\nVoiceBridge may be a control/service target rather than a normal handoff recipient.\n\nC. ROUTE + FOCUS\nOptional second action:\n\n\"Route + Focus\"\n\nThis should:\n- write the inbox handoff first\n- then request DevPad to focus/open the correct tile\n\nD. HOLD / DEFER\nAllow Don to leave a handoff un-routed without losing it.\n\nE. STATUS\nMinimum lifecycle:\n\nNEW\nPENDING\nROUTED\nACKNOWLEDGED\nDONE\nHELD\nFAILED\n\nDo not over-engineer this if a smaller state model is cleaner.\n\nF. AUDIT TRAIL\nRecord:\n- when item was routed\n- by whom (Don)\n- target seat\n- resulting inbox/handoff ID\n- whether focus/ping succeeded\n\n==================================================\n9. ROUTING RULE\n==================================================\n\nRouting must use stable seat identity.\n\nDo NOT route by:\n\n- PID\n- model name\n- provider\n- process order\n- window index\n- current runtime executable\n\nExample:\n\nrouteTarget = \"luce\"\n\nThen the system resolves:\n\nseat = Luce\n-> seat registry\n-> inbox destination\n-> tile/control target\n-> current active runtime/session if one exists\n\nThe seat registry is the stable control plane.\n\n==================================================\n10. DEV PAD INTEGRATION\n==================================================\n\nThe main DevPad already has a local server/API concept.\n\nmyDon Control should ideally communicate through a small local API surface.\n\nPossible conceptual endpoints:\n\nGET /api/seats\nGET /api/inventory\nGET /api/handoffs\nPOST /api/handoffs\nPOST /api/handoffs/:id/route\nPOST /api/seats/:seat/focus\nPOST /api/seats/:seat/ping\n\nThese endpoint names are illustrative only.\n\nDo not assume they already exist.\n\nArchitect the cleanest minimal contract.\n\nThe architecture should prefer:\n\nmyDon Control\n    -> Command Center local API\n    -> inbox MCP / routing layer\n    -> seat registry\n    -> DevPad focus/control\n\nrather than having myDon directly manipulate terminals.\n\n==================================================\n11. FOCUS VS PING VS WAKE\n==================================================\n\nThese should be separate concepts.\n\nFOCUS\n- bring the relevant DevPad tile/pane to attention\n- browser/UI action\n\nPING\n- notify the target seat/runtime that inbox work is available\n\nWAKE\n- start a runtime if it is not active\n\nV1 does not need full WAKE behavior.\n\nPreferred V1:\n\nhandoff delivery\n+\nfocus\n+\nvisible pending badge\n\nPING may initially be implemented as:\n- inbox refresh\n- visible indicator\n- optional lightweight control message\n\nDo not require terminal input injection in V1 unless the existing runtime already supports it safely.\n\n==================================================\n12. INBOX DELIVERY MODEL\n==================================================\n\nPreferred model:\n\nStructured handoff object\n\nExample conceptual schema:\n\n{\n  \"handoffId\": \"...\",\n  \"source\": \"don\",\n  \"targetSeat\": \"luce\",\n  \"subject\": \"Verify DevPad runtime binding\",\n  \"project\": \"GCE_DevPad_Command_Center\",\n  \"priority\": \"normal\",\n  \"status\": \"routed\",\n  \"context\": \"...\",\n  \"attachments\": [],\n  \"createdAt\": \"...\",\n  \"routedAt\": \"...\",\n  \"sourcePane\": \"nico\",\n  \"sourceTile\": 4\n}\n\nYou may propose a better schema.\n\nImportant:\n\nDo not encode model/provider as routing authority.\n\nModel/provider can be metadata only.\n\n==================================================\n13. CONTROL PAD UI CONCEPT\n==================================================\n\nDon wants something visually simple.\n\nPossible layout:\n\nLEFT / CENTER:\nPending handoff queue\n\nRIGHT / BOTTOM:\nSeat quick actions\n\nExample:\n\n[1 Coda]\n[2 Chiave]\n[3 Vittorio]\n\n[4 Nico]\n[5 VoiceBridge]\n[6 Luce]\n\n[7 Talon]\n[8 Keel]\n[9 Falcon]\n\nEach seat control may support:\n\nFOCUS\nHANDOFF\nINBOX\nSTATUS\n\nThe handoff queue should be faster to operate than opening terminals manually.\n\nDon wants low-friction visual routing.\n\n==================================================\n14. VOICEBRIDGE FUTURE INTEGRATION\n==================================================\n\nThe design should leave room for voice commands.\n\nExamples:\n\n\"send this to Luce\"\n\"route this to number 6\"\n\"focus number 4\"\n\"open Nico\"\n\"next handoff\"\n\"hold this\"\n\"send and next\"\n\nVoiceBridge should call the same routing API as the UI.\n\nDo not create separate logic for voice.\n\nUI and VoiceBridge should converge on the same command/control layer.\n\n==================================================\n15. CURRENT / FUTURE DEVPAD LAYOUT NOTES\n==================================================\n\nThe current main board is 3x3.\n\nA likely future visual extension may add one utility row beneath the 3x3:\n\nBottom-left:\nBedrock Mantle\n\nBottom-middle:\nopen / TBD\n\nBottom-right:\nKimi ad hoc\n\nThose utility/runtime tiles are not necessarily stable seats.\n\nThis is another reason routing should resolve by seat registry rather than screen position alone.\n\n==================================================\n16. SECURITY / SAFETY CONSTRAINTS\n==================================================\n\nThe design should avoid:\n\n- storing passwords\n- storing browser cookies\n- storing provider API secrets in the UI\n- embedding tokens in handoff payloads\n- exposing secrets through command-line arguments\n- letting the control pad execute arbitrary shell commands\n- letting model/provider identity determine seat authority\n- silently rerouting failed handoffs\n- silently substituting another seat/runtime\n\nIf routing fails:\nshow FAILED\nand keep the handoff visible.\n\n==================================================\n17. RELIABILITY PRINCIPLES\n==================================================\n\nThe architecture should prefer:\n\n- explicit state\n- deterministic routing\n- idempotent handoff sends where practical\n- clear failure states\n- auditability\n- easy local debugging\n- minimal dependencies\n- local-first operation\n- reversible changes\n- reuse of existing Command Center infrastructure\n\nDo not propose a distributed system if a local Node service is enough.\n\nDo not introduce Kafka, Redis, Kubernetes, or equivalent heavyweight infrastructure unless absolutely justified.\n\nThis is a personal local orchestration system.\n\n==================================================\n18. IMPLEMENTATION STYLE\n==================================================\n\nCurrent Command Center is already Node-based.\n\nPrefer compatibility with:\n\n- Node.js\n- local HTTP API\n- WebSocket only where useful\n- simple browser UI\n- existing seat registry\n- existing inbox MCP plumbing\n\nDo not require a framework rewrite.\n\nA small standalone web app or module is acceptable.\n\nArchitect whether myDon should be:\n\nOPTION A\nA second route/page hosted by the existing Command Center server\n\nOPTION B\nA separate lightweight local app/service\n\nOPTION C\nA separate frontend using the same Command Center API\n\nCompare these and recommend the cleanest path.\n\n==================================================\n19. KEY ARCHITECTURAL QUESTION\n==================================================\n\nThe central question is:\n\nWhat is the cleanest minimal architecture for a Don-controlled handoff/routing pad that:\n\n- reads pending handoffs\n- routes them by seat\n- writes into the existing inbox/MCP lane\n- records state\n- optionally focuses/pings the correct DevPad tile\n- supports future VoiceBridge commands\n- does not duplicate the main DevPad\n- preserves MODEL != SEAT\n\n==================================================\n20. WHAT I WANT FROM YOU\n==================================================\n\nProduce a practical architecture specification.\n\nPlease return:\n\n1. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\n2. RECOMMENDED ARCHITECTURE\n\n3. COMPONENT DIAGRAM\nASCII is fine.\n\n4. DATA FLOW\nShow:\nhandoff created\n-> queue\n-> Don routes\n-> inbox delivery\n-> DevPad target\n-> acknowledgement/status\n\n5. COMPONENT RESPONSIBILITIES\nFor:\n- myDon frontend\n- routing service/API\n- seat registry\n- inbox/MCP adapter\n- DevPad control adapter\n- VoiceBridge adapter\n- handoff state store\n\n6. HANDOFF DATA MODEL\nRecommend a concrete schema.\n\n7. API CONTRACT\nRecommend minimal endpoints.\n\n8. STATE MACHINE\nRecommend simple lifecycle/state transitions.\n\n9. FAILURE HANDLING\nEspecially:\n- target seat unavailable\n- DevPad unavailable\n- inbox write fails\n- duplicate send\n- focus fails\n- runtime offline\n\n10. UI STRUCTURE\nMinimal practical layout.\n\n11. VOICEBRIDGE FUTURE HOOK\nHow voice commands reuse the same API.\n\n12. SECURITY BOUNDARIES\n\n13. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES\nPrefer:\nPhase 1 = queue + route\nPhase 2 = route + focus\nPhase 3 = ping/status\nPhase 4 = voice integration\n\n14. FILE / MODULE STRUCTURE\nRecommend how to place this relative to:\nC:\\Users\\Dekan AI Brother\\GCE_DON\\command-center\\\n\n15. TEST PLAN\nSmall but serious.\n\n16. RISKS / OPEN QUESTIONS\n\n17. FINAL RECOMMENDATION\nState whether this should be:\n- embedded in Command Center\n- separate frontend with shared API\n- separate service\n\nBe concrete.\n\nDo not redesign the entire GCE system.\n\nDo not change seat doctrine.\n\nDo not assign models to seats.\n\nDo not invent agent autonomy.\n\nKeep Don explicitly in control of routing decisions.\n\nThe design goal is:\n\nDON SEES THE HANDOFF\n-> DON CLICKS THE TARGET\n-> HANDOFF ROUTES\n-> TARGET INBOX RECEIVES IT\n-> DEVPAD FOCUSES/PINGS THE RIGHT SEAT\n-> DON MOVES TO THE NEXT ITEM\n```\n\nThat should give GLM enough context to produce a **real architecture spec instead of generic dashboard bullshit**.\n\nThe core design question I want GLM to resolve is whether **myDon should be a separate frontend sharing the existing Command Center API**, or just another page/module inside the current server. My bias is **separate frontend, shared control plane/API**—that preserves the distinction between *work surface* and *orchestration surface* while avoiding another backend."]}, "create_time": 1790228325.529886, "end_turn": true, "id": "e7af21b6-677d-46f8-a086-bd2d2f56ca14", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "code_blocks": {"0": {"edited": false, "id": "mfpq0i", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}}, "content_references": [], "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finish_details": {"stop_tokens": [200002], "type": "stop"}, "gizmo_id": "g-p-6ab41b7c2620819187c626358eeb8a48", "is_complete": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "fff9049f-67af-422d-a8cd-235bbb71018d", "request_id": "413975a6-2af6-4531-a6f5-884fdb2f416d", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_result_groups": [], "story_events": [], "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "422e7a4a-fd25-4775-b60b-a0bf8ca12db6", "working_turn_id": "422e7a4a-