ts": [], "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "ea834b02-380f-4f5e-bae6-28e7c4d04930", "working_turn_id": "ea834b02-380f-4f5e-bae6-28e7c4d04930"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790229000.110908, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "user"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content_type": "multimodal_text", "parts": [{"asset_pointer": "sediment://file_000000001a6881f5b5a117c3bb1c1a60", "content_type": "image_asset_pointer", "fovea": null, "height": 1111, "metadata": {"asset_pointer_link": null, "container_pixel_height": null, "container_pixel_width": null, "dalle": null, "emu_omit_glimpse_image": null, "emu_patches_override": null, "generation": null, "gizmo": null, "is_no_auth_placeholder": null, "lpe_delta_encoding_channel": null, "lpe_keep_patch_ijhw": null, "sanitized": true, "segmentation": null, "watermarked_asset_pointer": null}, "size_bytes": 329990, "width": 2048}, "Holy shit. Holy shit, this makes Don so happy. Look at that shit. The identities load automatically.   Once we get you in there, we're good to go. "]}, "create_time": 1790229039.455, "end_turn": null, "id": "67bd5a38-d74b-4833-9661-641b9ef642cd", "metadata": {"attachments": [{"height": 1111, "id": "file_000000001a6881f5b5a117c3bb1c1a60", "is_big_paste": false, "library_file_id": "libfile_cc83e5a278208191aa30c2166708411d", "mime_type": "image/png", "name": "0ef4a21b-a846-4091-9e70-e313befe1944.png", "size": 329990, "source": "local", "width": 2048}], "can_save": false, "message_source": null, "request_id": "ca538124-b3e1-434e-8b83-1addb2bcb147", "selected_sources": [], "serialization_metadata": {"custom_symbol_offsets": []}, "turn_exchange_id": "52d5f364-bfb7-4d8b-a547-5a8aed6ea4c0", "working_turn_id": "52d5f364-bfb7-4d8b-a547-5a8aed6ea4c0"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790229062.113558, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["That’s the breakthrough.\n\nWhat you’ve proven in the screenshot is the part that actually mattered most:\n\n- **seat layout is working**\n- **identity homes are loading automatically**\n- **the seat-specific bootstrap/context is coming in**\n- **Vittorio is visibly loading the Kimi-side identity correctly**\n- **Nico is loading as Nico**\n- **Luce is in the right identity home**\n- **Chiave is in the right identity home**\n- Talon / Keel / Falcon are all landing in their proper seat directories\n\nThat means the DevPad is no longer just a terminal launcher. It is becoming an actual **identity-aware operating surface**.\n\nAnd yes: once Coda gets the persistent browser seat working correctly, the main board is basically at the point where you can start operating from it instead of juggling Windows terminals manually.\n\nThe remaining cleanup is much narrower now:\n\n**Coda browser seat → runtime/model bindings → polish.**\n\nAfter that, the interesting work shifts from “make DevPad function” to “make DevPad pleasant” — myDon controller, handoffs, quick folders, utility row, tighter layout, and voice navigation.\n\nThis screenshot is the first one where I’d say the concept is visibly real rather than aspirational."]}, "create_time": 1790229043.114279, "end_turn": true, "id": "161659a5-cd97-461c-873b-46f62992763c", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [], "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finish_details": {"stop_tokens": [200002], "type": "stop"}, "gizmo_id": "g-p-6ab41b7c2620819187c626358eeb8a48", "is_complete": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "f2847436-d505-4e4b-9cab-7ce9ee3f60b8", "request_id": "ca538124-b3e1-434e-8b83-1addb2bcb147", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_result_groups": [], "story_events": [], "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "52d5f364-bfb7-4d8b-a547-5a8aed6ea4c0", "working_turn_id": "52d5f364-bfb7-4d8b-a547-5a8aed6ea4c0"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790229061.47002, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "user"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Accepted on all four corrections. Each one is sound, and together they do something specific: they convert my open questions #1–#2 from \"things to check\" into **build-order constraints**, which is where they belonged. The revised position in one line:\r\n\r\n> **myDon is a UI + thin API over the existing inbox, not a system with its own queue. The myDon-side store is whatever minimum the existing inbox cannot represent — nothing more.**\r\n\r\nHere are the amendments to the spec, then the Phase 0 recon kit, since that's now the gating artifact.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Amendment 1 — The queue lives in the existing inbox if it can\r\n\r\nMy original spec assumed a myDon-owned queue store feeding the inbox. That's inverted. The canonical V1 flow is now:\r\n\r\n```\r\nexisting inbox (unrouted/pending pool)\r\n        -> Don reviews in /mydon\r\n        -> Don routes (click / key / later: voice)\r\n        -> routing service assigns the item into the target seat's inbox\r\n        -> ROUTED + audit record\r\n        -> [Phase 2 only] DevPad focuses the tile\r\n```\r\n\r\n`data/mydon/` is demoted from \"the store\" to \"the residue\": whatever the existing inbox genuinely cannot hold — most likely just routing decisions, focus results, and audit. If the inbox already covers identity and status well, that residue may be a single small file.\r\n\r\nThe spec's store section is now **conditional on the recon outcome**, with three branches:\r\n\r\n| Branch | Existing inbox supports... | myDon-side state |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| **A** | unrouted pool + item IDs + mutation | routing/audit record only — no queue store at all |\r\n| **B** | per-seat inboxes only, no unrouted pool | same as A, plus an unrouted holding lane *inside the existing inbox system* |\r\n| **C** | insert-only, no IDs/status/mutation | minimal fallback: single JSON file, deterministic IDs, queue mirrored on delivery |\r\n\r\nBranch A is the reviewer's \"even cleaner\" outcome: **existing inbox queue → Don routes → target inbox**, with myDon as pure surface.\r\n\r\nOne doctrine note on Branch B: an unrouted holding location must not masquerade as a seat. If it has to be registered somewhere, it's a non-routable holding lane, clearly marked, and it is never a valid route target. Seats receive; the holding lane waits.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Amendment 2 — One process, two surfaces, no ambiguity\r\n\r\nOption A stands, with the UX posture made explicit:\r\n\r\n- Main window: `http://localhost:4727/` — work surface\r\n- Controller window: `http://localhost:4727/mydon` — orchestration surface\r\n- Same-origin, same server, same control plane; distinct application feel — compact, dense, no terminal chrome, sized for a small window sitting alongside the terminal wall.\r\n\r\nOperationally this also means: one WebSocket bus, one seat registry read path, one auth posture (loopback), zero CORS. The separation is conceptual and visual, not infrastructural. That's the whole point of A.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Amendment 3 — Invariants over machinery\r\n\r\nCorrect, and worth saying plainly: monthly JSONL partitions, snapshots, projections, and compaction were more than V1 needs. The durable content of that section was never the event-sourcing pattern — it was the guarantees. Those survive as **implementation-independent acceptance criteria** that hold regardless of which branch Nico lands in:\r\n\r\n1. Every handoff has a deterministic ID, and that ID is present in the delivered inbox artifact.\r\n2. Route to seat X delivers exactly one item; repeat route to X is a visible no-op.\r\n3. Route to a different seat after routing is rejected — never silent reroute, never substitution.\r\n4. Failed delivery stays visible with its error; retry is explicit and manual.\r\n5. Every route records: who (don), when, handoff, target seat, delivery outcome, focus outcome.\r\n6. Queue and state survive process restart.\r\n7. The routing path contains no model/provider/runtime/PID. Display-only metadata lives elsewhere (the seat pad may show *Luce — Big Pickle — OpenCode — LIVE*; the route is still `targetSeat = luce`).\r\n8. Delivery success never depends on UI, focus, or runtime liveness.\r\n\r\nFor Branch C, the entire crash-safety story compresses to one rule Nico can implement any way he likes: **record intent before the side effect, and make the side effect findable by its ID.** On restart, an item with intent-but-no-confirmation gets probed in the target inbox by deterministic ID and resolved. At personal-system scale, that's the whole reconciliation mechanism. No projections required.\r\n\r\nStorage mechanics are now explicitly **Nico's call**, bounded by the eight invariants above. If JSONL falls out naturally from what exists, fine — but nothing in V1 *requires* it.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Amendment 4 — Locked phase gate\r\n\r\nThe phase sequence holds hard, and the Phase 1 acceptance test is exactly as stated, formalized:\r\n\r\n> One pending handoff visible in `/mydon` → Don clicks Luce (or presses `6`) → Luce's existing inbox contains exactly one item — the structured artifact, carrying the handoff's deterministic ID → myDon shows ROUTED → selection advances to the next item.\r\n\r\nPlus two cheap hardening checks at the same gate: kill and restart the server → queue intact; route the same handoff to Luce again → still exactly one inbox item.\r\n\r\nNothing in Phase 2 begins until this is green.\r\n\r\nOne scope clarification worth freezing now, since it's where the Windows focus rabbit hole actually lives: **V1 focus is in-DevPad tile focus only** — highlight the tile, switch the board view, badge the target. OS-level window raising and HWND manipulation are explicitly out of scope and stay out until the core loop is proven. Most of the focus hell is in that excluded zone.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Phase 0 — Inbox recon kit\r\n\r\nThe immediate artifact. Eight questions against the existing `ai-inbox` / inbox MCP, each with its decision attached:\r\n\r\n| # | Question | Decides |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| R1 | Do items have stable IDs? Can a writer supply one (upsert), or is every write an insert? | Whether idempotency leans on the inbox or needs a myDon guard |\r\n| R2 | **Can an item exist unrouted, or must a target seat be set at write time?** | Branch A / B / C — the single most important question |\r\n| R3 | Can an existing item's target or status be changed (assign/move), or are items immutable? | Route mechanics: assign-in-place (cleanest), move, or copy-with-ID |\r\n| R4 | What statuses exist? Writable externally? Room for held/routed/done, or a tags/flags field? | Where HELD and (Phase 3) ACK live |\r\n| R5 | Can a caller list unrouted items, list per-seat items, fetch by ID? | Whether the queue view is a pure projection (no store) |\r\n| R6 | What payload fields does an item carry — subject, body, project, priority, source, attachments-by-reference? Size limits? | The mapping table from the myDon handoff schema to the inbox artifact |\r\n| R7 | Push on write, or poll only? | UI refresh mechanics only |\r\n| R8 | Does the inbox already record who/when on writes? | Whether audit is one line in an existing log or myDon's residue |\r\n\r\nR2 and R3 together determine whether \"route\" is an **assignment** (item already in the inbox system, Don points it at a seat — the cleanest possible mechanic) or a **write** (myDon creates the artifact in the target seat's inbox). Prefer assignment if it exists.\r\n\r\nOutput of recon: the branch decision, the field mapping table, and the final store residue definition. That's a page, and it makes the implementation brief mechanical.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## What does not change\r\n\r\nOption A; `/mydon` route and `/api/mydon/*` namespace; the handoff schema and its doctrine rule (no model/provider/PID in the routing path — validation rejects them); idempotency and failure semantics; the six-state lifecycle; the keyboard model (`1–9`, `Shift+1–9`, `Enter`, `h`); VoiceBridge convergence on the same API; no second registry, no second messaging system, no agent autonomy, no silent anything.\r\n\r\n## Sequencing\r\n\r\nBuild start waits for Nico's current DevPad correction to land — no overlap between runtime-binding work and `/mydon` introduction. One option to note, and Don's call entirely: the recon is read-only inspection of existing inbox behavior and doesn't touch DevPad stabilization, so it can run early (or even be eyeballed directly against the inbox files) without violating that sequencing. Only the *build* holds.\r\n\r\nWith the recon filled in, the frozen principle set gains one line — **smallest store compatible with the existing inbox** — and the spec is ready to collapse into the Nico implementation brief as soon as DevPad is stable."]}, "create_time": 1790229137.541, "end_turn": null, "id": "2203a6e9-de3a-43c5-8dda-7f006710bf0c", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "message_source": null, "request_id": "19cb5086-20c1-4c67-9ba1-aba29107dd12", "selected_sources": [], "serialization_metadata": {"custom_symbol_offsets": []}, "turn_exchange_id": "166e4c2b-7cfe-497e-a0bb-f073d44076d5", "working_turn_id": "166e4c2b-7cfe-497e-a0bb-f073d44076d5"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790229154.066747, "weight": 1}], "moderation_results": [], "owner": null, "page_info": {"end_cursor": "2203a6e9-de3a-43c5-8dda-7f006710bf0c", "has_next_page": true, "has_previous_page": false, "start_cursor": "2451e5d5-0441-41fb-9ef8-35020ee5500c"}, "pinned_time": null, "plugin_ids": null, "safe_urls": ["https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B?chat_template=default&utm_sourc